registerRefData('nsw-cond-n-1359',{"id":1359,"pan":null,"ref":"DA/765/2021","address":"414 Old Maitland Road Mardi 2259","description":"Staged Community Title Subdivision Creating 246 Residential Lots Servicing, Road Works, Earthworks, Demolition & Landscaping","outcome":"refused","outcome_raw":"Refused","outcome_source":"planning_panels","determined":"2022-02-21","notice_url":"https://apps.planningportal.nsw.gov.au/prweb/PRRestService/DocMgmt/v1/PublicDocuments/DATA-WORKATTACH-FILE PEC-DPE-EP-WORK PPSHCC-85!20250602T010749.193 GMT","sha256":"5b779670e8e47aa330ec30eb2a7bcaf46402b5e25bb71bebbfeb1f86ab1e7b84","pages":80,"layout":"numbered","n":450,"fetched_at":"2026-08-16T23:19:06+00:00","file":"n/1359.js","source_page":"https://www.planningportal.nsw.gov.au/planning-panel/staged-community-title-subdivision-creating-246-residential-lots-servicing-road-works-earthworks-demolition-landscaping","lga":"CENTRAL COAST","conditions":[{"label":"1","code":null,"number":"1","title":"","text":"COMMISSIONER: The small township of Mardi is located inland from the central coast\nof NSW, a few kilometres from Wyong. To the west of Mardi lies the Old Maitland Road,\nalong which is located the Mardi dam that is a source of drinking water for the\ncommunity nearby.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"2","code":null,"number":"2","title":"","text":"To the north of Mardi Dam lies a large parcel of land that is proposed as a site for\ndevelopment, and which adjoins the Ourimbah State Forest to its north and east.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"3","code":null,"number":"3","title":"To this end, the Applicant in these proceedings, Stevens Holdings (Stevens), lodged","text":"development application no. DA/765/2021 with Central Coast Council on 23 July 2021\nseeking consent for subdivision of land into 246 lots (the original DA).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"4","code":null,"number":"4","title":"","text":"The DA was refused by the Hunter and Central Coast Regional Planning Panel on 21\nFebruary 2022, after which a request for Review under s 8.2 of the Environmental\nPlanning and Assessment Act 1979 (EPA Act) was lodged, subsequent to which the DA\nwas amended to reduce the subdivision to 219 lots.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"5","code":null,"number":"5","title":"","text":"Stevens appealed the refusal by filing an application in Class 1 of the court’s jurisdiction\non 20 February 2023, under s 8.7 of the EPA Act.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"6","code":null,"number":"6","title":"","text":"The Court granted leave to Stevens to amend the development application on a\nnumber of occasions prior to, and during the hearing.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"7","code":null,"number":"7","title":"","text":"On the fifth day of the hearing, Stevens sought an adjournment to prepare amended\nplans and other documents responsive to contentions pressed by the Council.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"8","code":null,"number":"8","title":"","text":"The Council did not oppose the application and likewise sought time to prepare a\nfurther amended statement of facts and contentions.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"9","code":null,"number":"9","title":"","text":"I granted the adjournment and directed that amended documents be filed and served\nby timeframes that were largely met by the parties.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"10","code":null,"number":"10","title":"","text":"I also record that the following amended plans and other documents for which Stevens\nwas granted leave were the subject of Notice of Motion, unopposed, dated 24\nSeptember 2024.\nSubdivision Biodiversity Development Assessment Report, Revision 10 dated\nSeptember 2024.\nServices Alignment Biodiversity Development Assessment Report, Revision 9,\ndated September 2024.\nSubdivision Arboricultural Assessment Report, Revision 4, dated September\n2024.\nServices Arboricultural Assessment Report, Revision 6, dated September 2024.\nBiodiversity Management Plan, Revision 9, dated September 2024.\nUpdated letter - Arborists Assessment of R5 and C2 zoned land to avoid indirect\nimpacts to C2 zoned land\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 4/80\nTree pruning letter - Arborist advice for tree pruning along Old Maitland Road to\nestablish traffic sightlines for proposed 414 Old Maitland Road residential\nsubdivision Mardi, NSW, dated 12 September 2024\nTree survey letter - Changes to tree assessment in arboriculture from survey of\ntrees for proposed 414 Old Maitland Road residential subdivision Mardi, NSW,\ndated 13 September 2024.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":4},{"label":"11","code":null,"number":"11","title":"","text":"I also note here that the Council approved the amending of the development application\nno. DA/765/2021 in accordance with s 38 of the Environmental Planning and\nAssessment Regulation 1979 (EPA Regulation) by those documents listed at [10],\nsubject to Stevens paying the Council’s costs thrown away as a result of the\namendments to the application pursuant to s 8.15(3) of the EPA Act, as agreed or\nassessed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"12","code":null,"number":"12","title":"","text":"It is also helpful to record here that while the hearing was initially allocated to\nCommissioner Horton and Acting Commissioner Moore, on 1 November 2024 the\nparties agreed in writing, and the Chief Judge concurred, to the proceedings being\nheard and disposed of by Commissioner Horton in accordance with s 30(3)(a) of the\nLand and Environment Court Act 1979 (LEC Act).\nThe proposal","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"13","code":null,"number":"13","title":"","text":"The site has a frontage to Old Maitland Road of approximately 1.14km and a combined\nsite area of around 223 hectares. The land on the site is currently used for agricultural\npurposes, with a mix of cleared land and native bushland.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"14","code":null,"number":"14","title":"","text":"In general terms, the proposal takes three essential forms:\n(1) Firstly, subdivision for the purposes of residential development on around 56\nhectares of the site.\n(2) Secondly, the reservation of around 140 hectares in what is known as ‘The\nStewardship site’, to be managed under a Biodiversity Stewardship Agreement.\n(3) Thirdly, an area of around 12 hectares identified for native vegetation\nregeneration under a Biodiversity Management Plan.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"15","code":null,"number":"15","title":"The proposed development, as characterised by the Council in the Amended Statement","text":"of Facts and Contentions filed 14 October 2024 (Exhibit 11), comprises:\n(1) Initial Torrens Title Subdivision to create two lots:\n(a) Proposed Lot 1 will contain all of the land for future development\nincluding the C3 Environmental Management Corridor and R5 large lot\nresidential land.\n(b) Proposed Lot 2 contains all of the land zoned C2 which is to be the\nsubject of a Stewardship Agreement.\n(2) A Development Lot Plan to create Lot 1 Community Association Property and 5\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 5/80\ndevelopment lots for further subdivision in stages.\n(3) The community title staged subdivision of proposed Lots 2-6 to create a total of\n182 residential lots in five (5) stages, including:\n(a) Stage 1: 48 community title lots\n(b) Stage 2: 21 community title lots\n(c) Stage 3: 50 community title lots\n(d) Stage 4: 50 community title lots\n(e) Stage 5: 13 lots in a neighbourhood scheme.\n(4) Infrastructure works internal and external to the site to create water, stormwater,\nsewer, electricity and NBN connections.\n(5) The construction of roads internal to the site and a new primary intersection with\nOld Maitland Road approximately 200m north of Collies Lane and a secondary\nemergency service access south of McPherson Road.\n(6) Earthworks, filling and flood mitigation works.\n(7) Removal of vegetation.\n(8) Landscaping.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":5},{"label":"16","code":null,"number":"16","title":"Servicing for the site will involve the following aspects occurring internally and","text":"externally to the site:\n(1) The construction of a new wastewater pump station on the Old Maitland Road\nfrontage and infrastructure works via Collies Lane to an existing connection on\nthe corner of Mildon Road and Gavenlock Road approximately 4km away (the\nServices Corridor).\n(2) Water pipe infrastructure works to connect to the existing water main adjacent to\nCouncil’s water treatment plant south of the site.\n(3) Connection to existing overhead power lines with new substations.\n(4) The construction of an internal road network.\n(5) All works are to be built stage-by-stage","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"17","code":null,"number":"17","title":"","text":"Additional works as part of the subdivision include:\n(1) Construction of an internal road network, including a flood free emergency\naccess point.\n(2) Dewatering and removal of existing ponds/farm dams and redirection,\nreconstruction and partial piping of 100 m of an unnamed creek and\nestablishment and retention of a 20m wide riparian zone on either side of the\ncreek for the section within the C3 zoned land through the central to north-\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 6/80\neastern portion of the site.\n(3) Bridge access to the Stage 5 neighbourhood scheme land over Deep Creek.\n(4) Implementation of stormwater and water treatment measures in each stage.\n(5) Landscaping works, including the establishment of open space areas and street\ntrees.\n(6) Fill of up to 4.69m to meet the 1% AEP flood level and a 500mm freeboard (with\nan allowance for climate change), including importing 256,629m of material.\n(7) Removal of vegetation outside of the C2 Environment Conservation zone.\nA Biodiversity Stewardship Site Agreement is proposed","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":6},{"label":"18","code":null,"number":"18","title":"","text":"A Planning Agreement requires the Stewardship Site at [14(2)] to be secured for\nconservation by a Biodiversity Stewardship Agreement (BSA) (Exhibit A, Tab T).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"19","code":null,"number":"19","title":"A Biodiversity Stewardship Site Assessment Report (BSSAR), prepared by AEP and","text":"dated March 2023 accompanies the development application (Exhibit A, Tab A20)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"20","code":null,"number":"20","title":"","text":"Stevens asserts (Exhibit M, folio 170) that the BSA will conserve and regenerate the\nfollowing ecological communities and threatened species present on the site:\n“Two (2) threatened ecological communities (TECs):\nSwamp Sclerophyll Forest on Coastal Floodplains, and\nRiver-flat Eucalypt Forest on Coastal Floodplains.\nRhodemnia rubescens (Scrub Trupentine)\nSyzygium paniculatum (Magenta Lilly Pilly)\nMelaleuca biconvexa (Biconvex Paperbark)\nLarge-eared Pied Bat (Chalinolobus dwyeri)\nSouthern Myotis (Myotis Macropus)\nGreen-thighed Frog (Litoria brevipalamata)”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"21","code":null,"number":"21","title":"Additionally, the BSSAR asserts that five plant community types (PCTs) are found","text":"within the Stewardship site, including:\n(1) Blackbutt - Narrow-leaved White Mahogany shrubby tall open forest of coastal\nranges, northern Sydney bioregion (PCT 684)\n(2) Blackbutt - Turpentine - Sydney Blue Gum mesic tall open forest on ranges of\nthe Central Coast (PCT 1568)\n(3) Swamp Mahogany - Flax-leaved Paperbark swamp forest on coastal lowlands\nof the Central Coast associated with the threatened ecological community:\nSwamp Sclerophyll Forest (SSF) on coastal floodplains of the New South Wales\nNorth Coast, Sydney Basin and South East Corner bioregions (PCT 1718).\n(4) Cabbage Gum - Forest Red Gum - Flax-leaved Paperbark swamp forest on\ncoastal lowlands of the Central Coast associated with the threatened ecological\ncommunity: River-flat Eucalypt Forest on coastal floodplains of the New South\n3\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 7/80\nWales North Coast, Sydney Basin and South East Corner bioregions (PCT\n1720)\n(5) Melaleuca biconvexa - swamp mahogany - cabbage palm swamp forest of the\ncentral coast, associated with the threatened ecological community: Swamp\nSclerophyll Forest (SSF) Forest on coastal floodplains of the New South Wales\nNorth Coast, Sydney Basin and South East Corner bioregions (PCT 1723)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":7},{"label":"22","code":null,"number":"22","title":"","text":"An email from the Credits Supply Taskforce at the Department of Planning and\nEnvironment dated 28 March 2023 (Exhibit B, Tab 2) confirms the application for the\nBSA was successful, and advises of certain arrangements required for its enactment.\nA Biodiversity Management Plan is proposed","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"23","code":null,"number":"23","title":"","text":"The Biodiversity Management Plan (BMP) (Exhibit M, Tab 5) identifies land in certain\nlocations on the site that are proposed for regeneration, across Stage 1 and Stage 5 of\nthe development. In broad terms, the locations, known as the BMP lands, include:\n(1) A corridor along the unnamed creek in the area zoned C3, in which a natural\nchannel is to be reinstated;\n(2) Areas to the north of the site in the vicinity of Deep Creek, between land zoned\nR5 and C2, including an area proposed to be modified to achieve vehicle\ncrossing;\n(3) An area located to the south west of the site for the proposed translocation of\ntwo species; Magenta Lilly Pilly and Scrub Turpentine; and\n(4) A 10m buffer zone to the perimeter of the site (10m buffer).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"24","code":null,"number":"24","title":"","text":"While more detailed maps are contained within the BMP, the locations and staging of\nthe BMP lands are most simply illustrated in Figure 9 of the Biodiversity Development\nAssessment Report (BDAR) prepared by AEP (Exhibit M, Tab 1), re-produced below:\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 8/80\nFigure 1: Map depicting the Biodiversity Management Plan Stages.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":8},{"label":"25","code":null,"number":"25","title":"","text":"The BDAR describes the intent of the BMP is to improve the overall quality of the\nvegetation by removing activities such as grazing by horses and cattle, and by\nproposing such activities as planting and weed management so as to arrest the\ndegrading of the site. (Exhibit M, folio 173)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"26","code":null,"number":"26","title":"","text":"Stevens proposes that the BMP Lands be managed for a period of 20 years, with full\nCouncil involvement after that time when the system will be in a state of natural\nregeneration providing ecological benefits to the lower reaches of the Tuggerah\ncatchment.\nThe site and its context","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"27","code":null,"number":"27","title":"","text":"The site is commonly known as 414 Old Maitland Road, Mardi, and comprises a total of\neight (8) allotments, including:\n(1) Lot 1, DP 120512\n(2) Lot 1, DP 554423\n(3) Lot A, DP 396415\n(4) Lot 1, DP 229970\n(5) Lot 1, DP 229971\n(6) Lot 101, DP 604655\n(7) Lot 36, DP 755249\n(8) Lot 41, DP 123953","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"28","code":null,"number":"28","title":"","text":"There are two waterways on the site, the character and classification of which are\ndisputed, that ultimately drain into the Wyong River. These are:\n(1) Deep Creek, which runs diagonally across the site from the south west to the\nnorth east through what is predominantly zoned C2 land.\n(2) Creek B, otherwise known as the ‘unnamed creek’, which runs through R5\nzoned land until it connects to Deep Creek in the north east of the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"29","code":null,"number":"29","title":"","text":"As a result of a planning proposal, the site was rezoned on 20 July 2018 under an\namendment to the Wyong Local Environmental Plan 2013 (WLEP).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"30","code":null,"number":"30","title":"","text":"At the same time a planning agreement on the site was executed that, at the time the\nappeal was filed with the Court, was in effect. The Planning agreement, dated October\n2016 (Exhibit A, Tab 18) identifies the following arrangement and requirements:\n“(a) Taking the necessary assessments, actions, arrangement, applications andprocesses to assess and secure land under a Biobanking Agreement in the Mardi areausing the Biobanking Assessment Methodology (or other NSW Government OffsettingScheme);\n(b) Undertake the necessary assessments, actions, arrangements, applications, andprocesses to obtain consent from relevant stage government agencies for anybiodiversity impacts arising from the Development of the land;\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 9/80\n(c) Undertake any works required to the local road network/infrastructure impacted bythe Development required by a Development Consent;\n(d) Construction and/or upgrade of the Potters Gully Fire Trail;\n(e) Construction and/or upgrade of the Mardi Dam West Fire Trail where it is located onthe Proponent’s land or a Crown Paper Road;\n(f) Provision of a monetary contribution to Council to construct the Mardi Dam West FireTrail where it is located on Council land;\n(g) Prevention of unauthorised access to Mardi Dam and environs; and\n(h) Granting of access to the Land by Council and the NSW Rural Fire Service to carryout bushfire protection and risk reduction activities.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":9},{"label":"31","code":null,"number":"31","title":"","text":"As a consequence of the rezoning at [29], the site is identified having three zone\ndesignations under the land use table in the WLEP. These are:\n(1) R5 Large Lot Residential\n(2) C2 Environmental Conservation\n(3) C3 Environmental Management","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"32","code":null,"number":"32","title":"","text":"An area of 49.5 hectares of the site is located within the R5 Large Lot Residential zone,\nas defined by the WLEP in which development such as subdivision and road\ndevelopment are permitted with consent, where consistent with the objectives of the R5\nzone that are:\n• To provide residential housing in a rural setting while preserving, and minimisingimpacts on, environmentally sensitive locations and scenic quality.\n• To ensure that large residential lots do not hinder the proper and orderly developmentof urban areas in the future.\n• To ensure that development in the area does not unreasonably increase the demandfor public services or public facilities.\n• To minimise conflict between land uses within this zone and land uses withinadjoining zones.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"33","code":null,"number":"33","title":"","text":"An area of around 140 hectares of the site is located within the C2 Environmental\nConservation zone, in which environmental protection works are permitted with\nconsent, with the objectives for development in the zone being as follows:\n• To protect, manage and restore areas of high ecological, scientific, cultural oraesthetic values.\n• To prevent development that could destroy, damage or otherwise have an adverseeffect on those values.\n• To protect endangered ecological communities, coastal wetlands and littoralrainforests.\n• To enable development of public works and environmental facilities if suchdevelopment would not have a detrimental impact on ecological, scientific, cultural oraesthetic values.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"34","code":null,"number":"34","title":"","text":"An area of around 12 hectares of the site is located within the C3 Environmental\nManagement zone, in which Environmental protection works, Flood Mitigation works,\nRecreation areas and Roads are permitted with consent, and where the objectives for\ndevelopment in the zone are:\n• To protect, manage and restore areas with special ecological, scientific, cultural oraesthetic values.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 10/80\n• To provide for a limited range of development that does not have an adverse effect onthose values.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":10},{"label":"35","code":null,"number":"35","title":"","text":"The proposed arrangement of development on the site when overlaid on the applicable\nland use zoning is best depicted on the following plan (Exhibit B, Tab A):\nFigure 2: Proposed arrangement of development and zoning on the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"36","code":null,"number":"36","title":"","text":"It is also relevant to record here that the development also proposes certain services\nnecessary to service the development within the road reserve along Collies Lane, Mardi\nRoad, Macpherson Road, Gavenlock Road and Old Maitland Road, and the upgrade of\nan intersection at the entry to the proposed subdivision on Old Maitland Road.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"37","code":null,"number":"37","title":"","text":"I note the alignment and proposed installation method for sewer and water mains within\nthe services corridor, whether by trenching or underboring, and including launch and\nretrieval pits, is depicted on plans prepared by Northrop (Exhibit A, Tab N)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"38","code":null,"number":"38","title":"","text":"The total area of road reserve initially identified as part of the development is 13.3\nhectares, of which around 4.11 hectares consists of native vegetation (Exhibit M, Vol 3,\nTab 2).\nPublic submissions","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"39","code":null,"number":"39","title":"The original DA was publicly notified between 20 August-24 September 2021, in","text":"response to which 53 submissions were received, of which I note 39 objected to the\nproposal and 14 were in support.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"40","code":null,"number":"40","title":"","text":"The Council’s bundle (Exhibit 2) records all public submissions received by the Council,\nincluding from September 2021, and those received between March and December\n2022, again in 2023, and six received in 2024.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"41","code":null,"number":"41","title":"","text":"The Council summarises the public submissions in the following categories:\n“o Significant adverse impact on biodiversity.\no Stormwater - water quality impacts.\no Use of neighbouring properties for the retention of water.\no Sewer pump station concern if fails what impacts from overflow.\no Traffic volumes.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 11/80\no Traffic safety (80km speed limit and no turning lanes) accident history in vicinity ofsite.\no Major change to the existing land use as NSW Department of Planning Central CoastRegional Strategy 2006- 2031 stated that \"the strategy will limit expansion of the urbanfootprint by protecting land west of the F3 (now M1) Freeway from further residentialdevelopment...\" and (page 7) \"the western half land supports agricultural productionand contains water supply catchments which must be protected from urban andresidential uses...\"in which all rural land west of the M1 was to be protected fromsubdivision. No suburban development west of the freeway due to environmentalimpacts.\no Adverse environmental impact on flora and fauna - Swamp Mahogany, BlackbuttTrees, koala habitat.\no Removal of critically endangered Scrub Turpentine.\no Indirect impacts to adjoining C2 zone (e.g. myrtle rust spread).\no Clearing/Modification of River Flat Eucalypt Forest, Swamp Sclerophyll, FreshwaterWetlands.\no Removal of Melaleuca bioconvexa, Removal of Magenta Lily Pilly.\no Removal of threatened fauna species foraging habitat.\no Previous DA objections from the RFS and the 100 year flood levels and habitat losshave been dismissed.\no Climate Change threatening all our infrastructure. No more clearing.\no Explosion of development in the past few years on the Northern end of the CentralCoast resulting in acres of bushland wiped out for residential housing.\no Cannot guarantee stewardship will protect the bush. Instead, it will introduce morepopulation next to bushland creating more usage, more rubbish, more pets, peopleunknowingly impacting on threatened species.\no Threat to the vulnerable Green-thighed Frog. Three breeding locations have beenidentified within the development footprint and one just beyond the development edge.Removal of floodplain breeding habitat connectivity be cut across the valley floor.\no Flooding occurs on the site. Evacuation is an issue.\no Further studies required to ensure no environmental impact.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":11},{"label":"42","code":null,"number":"42","title":"","text":"The hearing commenced with an onsite view at which the Court, in the company of the\nlegal representatives, the landowner and experts, heard an oral submission from a\nrepresentative of the Central Coast Community Better Planning Group (Exhibit 9) after\nwhich the Court was taken to five locations on the site, by both vehicle and by walking\nof the site between certain locations.\nThe biodiversity values of the site","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"43","code":null,"number":"43","title":"","text":"While the Site’s biodiversity values are disputed, it is commonly held that the following\nplant community types (PCT’s) are found on the site the subject of the proposed\nsubdivision:\n(1) Blackbutt - Narrow-leaved White Mahogany shrubby tall open forest of coastal\nranges, northern Sydney bioregion (PCT 684)\n(2) Blackbutt - Turpentine - Sydney Blue Gum mesic tall open forest on ranges of\nthe Central Coast (PCT 1568)\n(3) Swamp Mahogany - Flax-leaved Paperbark swamp forest on coastal lowlands\nof the Central Coast associated with the threatened ecological community:\nSwamp Sclerophyll Forest (SSF) on coastal floodplains of the New South Wales\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 12/80\nNorth Coast, Sydney Basin and South East Corner bioregions (PCT 1718).\n(4) Cabbage Gum - Forest Red Gum - Flax-leaved Paperbark swamp forest on\ncoastal lowlands of the Central Coast associated with the threatened ecological\ncommunity: River-flat Eucalypt Forest on coastal floodplains of the New South\nWales North Coast, Sydney Basin and South East Corner bioregions (PCT\n1720)\n(5) Melaleuca biconvexa - swamp mahogany - cabbage palm swamp forest of the\ncentral coast, associated with the threatened ecological community: Swamp\nSclerophyll Forest (SSF) Forest on coastal floodplains of the New South Wales\nNorth Coast, Sydney Basin and South East Corner bioregions (PCT 1723)\n(6) Typha rushland, associated with the threatened ecological community:\nFreshwater Wetlands on coastal floodplains of the New South Wales North\nCoast, Sydney Basin and South East Corner bioregions (PCT 1737)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":12},{"label":"44","code":null,"number":"44","title":"","text":"I note here that the Typha rushland cited above was initially excluded from the\nassessment of PCTs on the site given the location of such vegetation was within water\nbodies defined as former farm dams, which are not considered part of the PCT.\nHowever, for reasons that are not pertinent here, Typha rushland has been included,\nbut for which offset within the Stewardship site is not necessary. Instead, Stevens\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 13/80\nproposes that the regeneration of a naturalised drainage channel will reinstate an\naquatic ecosystem with bends, pools and riffles that would function similar to that of\nPCT 1737. (Exhibit M, folio 171)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":13},{"label":"45","code":null,"number":"45","title":"","text":"It is also relevant to record those plant community types (PCT’s) found on the site the\nsubject of the proposed services and intersection works, described at [36]-[38], that\ninclude:\n(1) Swamp Mahogany - Flax-leaved Paperbark swamp forest on coastal lowlands\nof the Central Coast (PCT 1718)\n(2) Melaleuca biconvexa - swamp mahogany - cabbage palm swamp forest of the\ncentral coast (PCT 1723)\n(3) Blackbutt - Turpentine - Sydney Blue Gum mesic tall open forest on ranges of\nthe Central Coast (PCT 1568)\n(4) Blackbutt - Rough-barked Apple - Turpentine - ferny tall open forest of the\nCentral Coast (PCT 1564)\n(5) Rough-barked Apple - Forest Oak - Grey Gum grassy woodland on sandstone\nranges of the Sydney Basin (PCT 1557)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"46","code":null,"number":"46","title":"","text":"Of those PCTs identified by Stevens on the site, two are endangered ecological\ncommunities (EECs):\n(1) Swamp Sclerophyll Forest (SSF), and\n(2) River Flat Eucalypt Forest (RFEF).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"47","code":null,"number":"47","title":"","text":"Of particular relevance to these proceedings is the likely impact on three species:\n(1) Rhodemnia rubescens, or Scrub Turpentine\n(2) Melaleuca biconvexa, or Biconvex paperbark,\n(3) Syzygium paniculatum, or Magenta Lilly Pilly.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 14/80\nRegular slashing of the site occurs","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":14},{"label":"48","code":null,"number":"48","title":"","text":"The Council contends that as the effects of slashing areas of open grassland is evident\nfrom aerial photography, such activity reduces the Vegetation Integrity on the site in a\nmanner not addressed in the Biodiversity Development Assessment Report (BDAR)\nthat accompanies the DA.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"49","code":null,"number":"49","title":"","text":"As a result, the Vegetation Integrity Score (VIS) is artificially suppressed by such\nactivity and offset credits, assessed under the Biodiversity Conservation Act 2016 (BC\nAct), are under-estimated, and so Stevens would obtain a benefit by activity that is, or\nwas, unlawful.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"50","code":null,"number":"50","title":"Floristic surveys undertaken by Stevens in areas of cleared land record the","text":"representation of native vegetation at less than 15% (Exhibit M, Tab 1, folio 23), which\nultimately informed the extent of further surveys and determined the VIS.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"51","code":null,"number":"51","title":"","text":"The affidavit of Lawrence John Denton dated 29 July 2024, read unopposed, attests\nthat:\n(1) The land has been used for the agistment of cattle since 1997.\n(2) On or about April 1998, the agreement with the owner of the cattle agisted on\nthe property was terminated, after which the landowner populated the site with\nits own cattle until 2005, during which the property was slashed on an annual\nbasis.\n(3) Around 2005, the landowner entered into another agreement to agist cattle on\nthe property. From 2005 to 2009 or 2010, a portion of the property was regularly\nslashed until a change in directorship of the landowner arrangements coincided\nwith a further agistment agreement in November 2012 that continued until 2017.\nSlashing continued throughout this period, as did mulching of the land between\n2017 and 2018.\n(4) Between 2018 and 2022, slashing continued on the site, undertaken by others,\nafter which the landowner has continued to slash.\n(5) In summary, the property has been slashed on a regular basis between 2017\nand today, at intervals of between 9-12 months.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"52","code":null,"number":"52","title":"","text":"Furthermore, the evidence of Mr Denton is that the purpose of the slashing and\nmulching of the property has been for the purpose of reducing fire risk, to maintain or\nimprove the quality of the pasture available to stock, and to manage safety risks\nassociated with the management of the pasture and stock. For example, as holes,\ndrainage lines and other obstacles occur on the site, it is important that grass on the\nsite is managed to avoid accidents that have occurred in the past.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"53","code":null,"number":"53","title":"","text":"The Council contends in the Amended Statement of Facts and Contentions (Exhibit 11)\nthat slashing of the sort deposed is visible on aerial photography of the site since at\nleast 2010, prior to the site being rezoned as R5 Large Lot Residential, C2\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 15/80\nEnvironmental Conservation and C3 Environmental Management, and that aerial\nphotography suggests that the slashing occurs on a regular basis.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":15},{"label":"54","code":null,"number":"54","title":"","text":"The zoning of land as R5 Large Lot Residential, C2 Environmental Conservation and\nC3 Environmental Management means that Part 5A of the Local Land Services Act\n2013 (LLS Act) no longer applies and instead Part 2.2 of the State Environmental\nPlanning Policy (Biodiversity and Conservation) 2021 applies in relation to clearing and\nvegetation protection, with no savings provision for Part 5A of the LLS Act to continue.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"55","code":null,"number":"55","title":"","text":"Chapter 3.6 of the Wyong Development Control Plan 2013 (WDCP), in force at the time\nof lodgement, applies to land zoned R5, C2 and C3 and applies to the protection of\nvegetation. The exemptions listed under Section 3.2 of the WDCP largely do not apply\nto the slashing activities on the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"56","code":null,"number":"56","title":"","text":"Furthermore, as the site is not within 50m of a dwelling, what is commonly known as\nthe ‘10/50 rule’ does not apply.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"57","code":null,"number":"57","title":"","text":"The upshot of all this is twofold:\n(1) Firstly, that Stevens has not provided an approval pathway in which the slashing\nis able to occur. As such, the Applicant is not able to obtain benefit from the\nunapproved works.\n(2) Secondly, the BDAR must assess the site in a condition as if the slashing had\nnot occurred. This will result in the need to consider higher Vegetation Integrity\nScores (VIS) in some, if not all, areas captured by the BDAR to ensure that no\nbenefit is obtained from the unapproved slashing works.\nStevens’ submissions on slashing works","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"58","code":null,"number":"58","title":"","text":"The primary submission of Stevens is that the site was largely cleared from at least\n1941 when the earliest aerial images are taken, and remained in that state until 1991,\nwhen aerial images contained in the First Wilkie Affidavit (Exhibit F) depict the land in a\nsimilar state, but for some re-growth.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"59","code":null,"number":"59","title":"","text":"The particular relevance of this history to the clearing on the site is twofold;\n(1) Firstly, that clearing and slashing of the land was not undertaken by Stevens, to\nthe extent a benefit is now sought in respect of consent for development on the\nland; and\n(2) Secondly, because the relevant legislative framework that applies to land deems\nagricultural use of the land to be an existing use under s 4.66 of the EPA Act, in\nwhich slashing is permitted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"60","code":null,"number":"60","title":"","text":"While Stevens set out a detailed chronology in the evolution of the legislative\nframework, the following summary would appear to suffice:\n(1) Successive aerial images taken between 1941 and September 1991 support a\nview that the land has been substantially cleared throughout that time for the\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 16/80\npurposes of agriculture.\n(2) The use of the land for grazing remained permissible without consent until 20\nJuly 2018 when the current zoning came into force with the enactment of the\nWLEP.\n(3) However, the continued use of the land for agriculture, involving grazing and\nslashing of the land, was an existing use that has remained in effect as a\ncommon thread through all of the various permutations of legislative change,\nthat has not be derogated by operation of State Environmental Planning Policy\nNo 46 - Protection and Management of Native Vegetation (Native Vegetation\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 17/80\nSEPP), or by the Native Vegetation Conservation Act 1997 (Native Vegetation\nAct) that otherwise required consent for clearing on land to which the Act applied\nwhen it came into force on 1 January 1998.\n(4) The transitional provisions contained in the Native Vegetation Act saved\nexemptions contained in the Native Vegetation SEPP so that the Native\nVegetation Act did not operate to require consent for the clearing of vegetation\notherwise permitted by the Native Vegetation SEPP.\n(5) On 1 December 2005, the regime changed again with the commencement of the\nNative Vegetation Act 2003 (the 2003 Act) that prohibited clearing of native\nvegetation without consent. However, s 9(2)(b) of the 2003 Act permitted the\nclearing of native vegetation where that vegetation was re-growth that had re-\ngrown since 1 January 1990.\n(6) Stevens submits that images contained in the Wilkie Affidavit depict vegetation\nthat had regrown since 1 January 1990.\n(7) On 25 August 2017, the 2003 Act was repealed, and the management of native\nvegetation became the preserve of the Local Land Services Act 2013 (the LLS\nAct), which sought to designate land as either exempt land (Category 1) or\nregulated land (Category 2) under Part 5A of the LLS Act, so defined at s 60D.\n(8) As the designation suggests, s 60E provides that Category 1 land is land where\nthe clearing of native vegetation is not regulated.\n(9) Section 60F(1) of the LLS Act establishes certain transitional arrangements that\nare in place prior to the publishing of a ‘Native vegetation regulatory map’, and\nwhere the categorisation of land is to be construed, at subs (4), by what a\nreasonable person would believe about the land being designated in that\ncategory.\n(10) Stevens relies on a map titled ‘Transitional Native Vegetation Regulatory Map’,\npublished by the Department of Environment and Heritage in August 2017, as\nevidence the site is category 1 land that is exempt from regulation in respect of\nland clearing (Exhibit B, Tab 10).\n(11) Section 60H(1)(a) provides that category 1 land is so designated if the land was\ncleared of native vegetation as at 1 January 1990, or at (b) the land was lawfully\ncleared of native vegetation between 1 January 1990 and the commencement of\nPart 5A of the LLS Act or, at (2)(a), the land contains low value conservation\nvalue grasslands.\n(12) Whilever the land carries the designation of Category 1 land, the land is exempt\nland and the proscriptions at Divisions 4, 5 and 6 of the LLS Act do not apply.\n(13) Further change was on the way yet, and as stated at [29], on 20 July 2018, the\nland the subject of the development application was zoned R5, C2 and C3 by\nadoption of Amendment No.17 of the WLEP. In being so designated, the land fell\nwithin the definition of urban areas of the state to which State Environmental\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 18/80\nPlanning Policy (Vegetation in Non-Rural Areas) 2017 (Non-Rural Vegetation\nSEPP) applied, at that time, pursuant to s 60A(a) of the LLS Act, and was no\nlonger subject to the provisions of the LLS Act.\n(14) That said, Stevens submits that exceptions applied under the Non-Rural\nVegetation SEPP such that no permit or other approval was required for reasons\nthat follow:\n(a) Part 5 is entitled ‘Clearing of native vegetation on primary production land\nin Zones R5, C2, C3 and E4 that does not require permit or approval’.\nClause 30 states that if the land, firstly, was identified on the ‘Allowable\nClearing Map’ and secondly, if that land was used for primary production\nas defined at s10AA the Land Tax Management Act 1956 (LTM Act), and\nfell within the zones nominated, then clearing of native vegetation for\nmaintenance of fences and farm tracks, water supply and other purposes\nwas permitted without a permit or approval by virtue of those provisions\ncontained at cll 31-35.\n(b) The provision at cl 36 of the Non-Rural Vegetation SEPP is virtually\nidentical, and applies to clearing in zones R5, C3 and E4 if likewise\nshown on the Allowable Clearing Map and is land subject to the LTM Act.\nThe provisions at cll 37-40 permit clearing of native vegetation for\nenvironmental\n(c) Stevens submits the site meets both criterion because:\n(i) The site is located within the area shown to the west of the M1\nMotorway and identified as ‘Allowable Clearing land’ on the\n‘Allowable Clearing Map’ (Exhibit B, Tab 9).\n(ii) The use of the site for the grazing of cattle is clearly consistent\nwith the definition of land used for primary production at s 10AA of\nthe LTM Act - a submission supported by an affidavit read,\nunopposed, on the final day of the hearing, sworn by Mr Denton\n(the Second Affidavit).\n(d) Next, Stevens submits that, in the alternative, even absent the\nexceptions to a permit or approval under Part 5 of the Non-Rural\nVegetation SEPP, the continuing use of land is an existing use under s\n4.65(a) of the EPA Act that is not prevented from continuance by s\n4.66(1) of the EPA Act.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":16},{"label":"61","code":null,"number":"61","title":"","text":"I accept the chronology of events at the centre of submissions by Stevens, and the\nargument advanced that the slashing of the land is a function of primary production that\nhas continued on the site following its clearing sometime at or before 1941.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"62","code":null,"number":"62","title":"","text":"In written submissions from the parties, the Court is asked to follow the decision in\nWhite v Ballina Shire Council [2021] NSWLEC 1468 (White), citing Telstra Corporation\nLtd v Hornsby Shire Council (2006) 67 NSWLR 256; [2006] NSWLEC 133 to\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 19/80\nsummarise the circumstances in which the precautionary principle might be applied.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":19},{"label":"63","code":null,"number":"63","title":"Nevertheless, Stevens submits that the factual circumstances in White are clearly","text":"distinguishable. In that case, unlike here, the applicant had pleaded guilty to the\nunlawful clearing. It was the applicant’s own wrong, in that they had sought to benefit\nfrom the unlawful clearing by assessing the impacts of a proposed upgraded track from\na baseline set by their own unlawful clearing.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"64","code":null,"number":"64","title":"Notwithstanding its position at [57(1)] that may infer otherwise, in closing submissions,","text":"the Council confirms that it does not assert that the clearing undertaken on the site was\nunlawful, nor rely on the decision in White due to any unlawfulness.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"65","code":null,"number":"65","title":"","text":"Instead, the Council merely submits that a consequence of the clearing is the\nsuppression of the VIS, and the Court should give significant weight to Dr McLean’s\nopinion that the precautionary principle is appropriately invoked to weigh against the\ngrant of consent because the Court does not have an accurate assessment of the\nvegetation integrity before it. Such is the contention summarised at [57(2)].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"66","code":null,"number":"66","title":"","text":"Stevens also submits that any assessment of the site that assumes slashing had not\noccurred can only be a speculation on an assumed, and not an actual, state of affairs.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"67","code":null,"number":"67","title":"","text":"I accept the history of this land is one of farming, including the grazing of cattle and of\nland management practices consistent with that use, and that the VIS arises from that\nchronology and use.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"68","code":null,"number":"68","title":"","text":"A consequence of that chronology and use is the degrading of the vegetation on the\nsite in those areas that have been cleared for a substantial period of time, and has so\ndegraded the PCT that may have historically occupied those areas.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"69","code":null,"number":"69","title":"","text":"In arriving at this conclusion, I accept the summary provided by AEP in a letter dated 7\nMarch 2024 (Exhibit A, Tab V) that the Native Vegetation Extent in the Biodiversity\nAssessment Method (BAM) plots recorded an assessment of less than 15%, and is\ndominated by exotic grasses.\nThe Biodiversity Development Assessment Report does not accurately quantify the\nextent of native vegetation on the site","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"70","code":null,"number":"70","title":"","text":"In addition to the Council’s contention that Stevens has not correctly calculated the VIS\nfor the site the subject of the proposed subdivision, the Council also argues that the\necosystem credits in respect of flora and fauna required to offset the impact of\ndevelopment on biodiversity are likewise in error.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"71","code":null,"number":"71","title":"The executive summary of the Subdivision BDAR records the following credits accruing","text":"due to flora species on the site:\nBiconvex Paperbark - 714 credits\nScrub Turpentine - 195 credits\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 20/80\nMagenta Lilly Pilly - 20 credits, notably exceeding the agreement of the experts\nthat a condition of consent requiring the retirement of 11 credits be applied\n(Exhibit 12, par 7)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":20},{"label":"72","code":null,"number":"72","title":"","text":"The BDAR lists four fauna species recorded within the subject site that will generate\ncredits to be retired under the Biodiversity Offset Scheme. Those species are:\n(1) Green-thighed frog - 372 credits\n(2) Large-eared Pied Bat - 1069 credits\n(3) Southern Myotis - 297 credits\n(4) South-eastern Glossy Black Cockatoo - 17 credits","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"73","code":null,"number":"73","title":"","text":"I note here that the South-eastern Glossy Black Cockatoo was initially detected on the\nsite, but as breeding behaviour for the Cockatoo was not found, the BDAR records the\nadvice of the Biodiversity Conservation Trust resulted in the removal of the species\nfrom the calculation of Biodiversity Offset Credits. Nevertheless, the credits are retired.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"74","code":null,"number":"74","title":"","text":"As stated earlier, two BDARs have been prepared in support of the proposal. One\nBDAR assesses the characteristics and impact of the site proposed for subdivision\n(Exhibit M, Tab 1), and another assesses the characteristics and impact resulting from\nthe services required by the site, that are proposed to be undergrounded in the road\nreserve as cited earlier at [36]-[38].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"75","code":null,"number":"75","title":"The Subdivision BDAR records the following ecosystem credits accruing by virtue of","text":"the residual impacts of the proposed services corridor and intersection works:\n(1) Blackbutt - Rough-barked Apple - Turpentine - ferny tall open forest of the\nCentral Coast (PCT 1564): 9 credits\n(2) Blackbutt - Turpentine - Sydney Blue Gum mesic tall open forest on ranges of\nthe Central Coast (PCT 1568): 53 credits","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"76","code":null,"number":"76","title":"A Biodiversity Credit Assessment Report, prepared by AEP (Exhibit M, Appendix G),","text":"supports the BDARs and calculates the biodiversity offset credits associated with the\nproposal, and that are to be retired in stages.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"77","code":null,"number":"77","title":"","text":"In s 6.12 of the BC Act, a BDAR is described as follows:\n6.12 Biodiversity development assessment report\nFor the purposes of the biodiversity offsets scheme, a biodiversity developmentassessment report is a report prepared by an accredited person in relation to proposeddevelopment or activity that would be authorised by a planning approval, or proposedclearing that would be authorised by a vegetation clearing approval, that-\n(a) assesses in accordance with the biodiversity assessment method the biodiversityvalues of the land subject to the proposed development, activity or clearing, and\n(b) assesses in accordance with that method the impact of proposed development,activity or clearing on the biodiversity values of that land, and\n(c) sets out the measures that the proponent of the proposed development, activity orclearing proposes to take to avoid or minimise the impact of the proposed development,activity or clearing, and\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 21/80\n(d) specifies in accordance with that method the number and class of biodiversitycredits that are required to be retired to offset the residual impacts on biodiversityvalues of the actions to which the biodiversity offsets scheme applies.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":21},{"label":"78","code":null,"number":"78","title":"","text":"A BDAR is to be considered by the consent authority, or the Court on appeal, when\nassessing the likely impact on biodiversity values, in accordance with s 7.13 of the BC\nAct in the following terms:\n7.13 Development other than State significant development or infrastructure\n(1) This section applies to an application for development consent under Part 4 of theEnvironmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979 that is required under Division 2 tobe accompanied by a biodiversity development assessment report, except-\n(a) an application for development consent for State significant development, or\n(b) an application for a complying development certificate.\n(2) The consent authority, when determining in accordance with the EnvironmentalPlanning and Assessment Act 1979 any such application, is to take into considerationunder that Act the likely impact of the proposed development on biodiversity values asassessed in the biodiversity development assessment report that relates to theapplication. The consent authority may (but is not required to) further consider underthat Act the likely impact of the proposed development on biodiversity values.\n…","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"79","code":null,"number":"79","title":"","text":"In summary, the Council contends that:\n(1) The extent of native and exotic vegetation is incorrectly mapped in the BDAR.\n(2) Plant species nominated for removal are misidentified.\n(3) An assessment of the likely impact of tree removal along Creek B that is\nproposed to be realigned is proposed to be deferred to later detailed design,\nprecluding a proper assessment of the likely impact.\n(4) Surveys of fauna such as for the large forest owls, Squirrel Glider, Common\nPlanigale, Green-thighed frog, Gang Gang Cockatoo and Little Lorikeet are\nincomplete.\n(5) Assessment of the extent of incursion by the proposed development into the\ntree protection zones (TPZ) of existing trees proposed for retention is so unclear\nthat those trees likely to require removal would result in a shortfall in the number\nof ecosystem credits.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"80","code":null,"number":"80","title":"","text":"In considering the biodiversity values of the site, the Court was assisted by the\nfollowing experts in ecology and arboriculture:\nDr Chris McLean, ecologist on behalf of the Council\nMs Natalie Black, ecologist on behalf of Stevens\nMr Warwick Muir, arborist on behalf of Stevens","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"81","code":null,"number":"81","title":"","text":"The experts conferred, to varying degrees, in the preparation of three joint expert\nreports.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"82","code":null,"number":"82","title":"","text":"Stevens objected to the tender of the ecology joint expert report (Exhibit 5) on the\ngrounds that the Council’s ecology expert had not conformed to the Court Policy on\njoint conferencing, had not entered into a genuine dialogue within the conclave, and\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 22/80\nthat data on which the Council’s expert relies was provided to the experts for Stevens\nso late as to be prejudicial.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":22},{"label":"83","code":null,"number":"83","title":"After hearing submissions on the objections taken, the Court directed that those","text":"paragraphs within the joint report that flowed from unilateral actions taken by the\nCouncil’s expert in the form of individual investigations, taken within the period of the\nconclave, be struck, but also considered other aspects objected to, to be within the\nscope of the contentions as drafted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"84","code":null,"number":"84","title":"","text":"The Court directed that the experts, who had not conferenced in person, so do and file\nand serve a further supplementary joint report prior to commencement of the hearing\nthe following day.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"85","code":null,"number":"85","title":"","text":"A further supplementary joint report was served but not filed the following day, and\nrecorded areas of agreement between the experts. However, it also contained\nparagraphs acknowledged by the Council to have been excluded by the Court’s\ndirections on the day prior.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"86","code":null,"number":"86","title":"","text":"In any event, as stated earlier, the Court adjourned part heard with leave granted to\nStevens to prepare an amended BDAR, BMP, AIA and certain other documents. What\nalso flowed from the amending of those documents were directions made on 24\nSeptember 2024 for experts to confer further and file and serve a further supplementary\njoint expert report on 4 November 2024.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"87","code":null,"number":"87","title":"","text":"It was necessary to rule on further objections made by Stevens, with certain\nparagraphs agreed between the parties to be struck from what was then marked Exhibit\n12.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"88","code":null,"number":"88","title":"","text":"It is commonly held that the preparation of the BDARs and the BSSAR should be\nundertaken independently, so that the assessment of biodiversity impact and\nassessment of resultant offset credits can be considered independently.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"89","code":null,"number":"89","title":"","text":"Preparing a BDAR requires assessment of flora and fauna using a methodology set out\nin the BAM Manual, which is supported by an operational manual, and requiring\ninterface with an online tool known as ‘BAM-C’.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"90","code":null,"number":"90","title":"","text":"For what might be called ‘quality assurance’ reasons, the BAM-C is a secure portal.\nUsers of the portal are registered, and access can be provided to consent authority on\na ‘read only’ basis, as it is in this case inasmuch as the Council is able to access the\n‘back end’ of inputs into the BAM-C by those preparing the BDAR.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"91","code":null,"number":"91","title":"","text":"Where questions arise in using the BAM-C tool, the Department of Environment and\nPlanning (the Department) offers users a ‘help desk’ known as the BOS Helpdesk (the\nhelp desk) and publishes Circulars to advise assessors on updates and provide\nguidance on frequently asked questions.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"92","code":null,"number":"92","title":"","text":"Dr McLean is of the view that insufficient independence was brought to the preparation\nof the BDAR for subdivision and the BSSAR because data from the BAM-C suggests\nBAM plots used in the BDAR and BSSAR were undertaken by the same individuals.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"93","code":null,"number":"93","title":"","text":"In both her written and oral evidence, Ms Black explains a process by which work\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 23/80\ninitially undertaken by a different ecology practice was transferred, in the case of the\nBSSAR, to a Mr Ian Benson, while responsibility for the preparation of the BDARs was\ngiven to Ms Black.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":23},{"label":"94","code":null,"number":"94","title":"","text":"While this was the case, it is Mr Benson’s name that appears as lead input into both the\nBSSAR and BDAR on the BAM-C software interface. Ms Black explains assistance was\nsought from the help desk. On the basis of email correspondence at par 486 of the\nsupplementary joint report (Exhibit 12), I accept the most likely reason for the identical\nnames being entered into the BAM-C program is the reason provided by Mr Phil Wood\nin an email dated 5 July 2024, which is due to a setting in the software interface that\nwas not selected.\nThe extent of native and exotic vegetation is incorrectly mapped in the BDAR.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"95","code":null,"number":"95","title":"The Subdivision BDAR records the chronology of field surveys and BAM plots","text":"completed on behalf of Stevens. Field surveys informed the location and number of\nBAM plots, with initial mapping of PCTs updated following ‘ground-truthing’ depicted in\nthe Subdivision BDAR at Figure 13, reproduced below.\nFigure 3: Map showing updated zones of Plant Community Types.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"96","code":null,"number":"96","title":"","text":"The ecology experts engage at length, in both their written and oral evidence, on the\ntopic of BAM plots undertaken on the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"97","code":null,"number":"97","title":"","text":"In general terms, Dr McLean queries the veracity of outputs from a number of BAM\nplots. This is variously because differing values were derived from BAM plots\nundertaken close to one another, as was the case in BAM plot No.38 or because\nrecords varied from the observations made by Dr McLean, as in the case of BAM plot\nNo.39 or No. 43, being the same plot for reasons that are not relevant, and in a number\nof other BAM plots Dr McLean completed (Exhibit 12, p 18).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"98","code":null,"number":"98","title":"","text":"In one BAM plot undertaken by Dr McLean, a VIS of 14.8 is recorded, noted by Dr\nMcLean to be a figure close to a score of 15, after which ecosystem credits are\ngenerated, and so it is speculated that areas in the vicinity of the BAM plot undertaken\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 24/80\nby Dr McLean might also achieve a VIS of 15. Furthermore, Dr McLean also notes that\nif BAM plot outputs completed by him are averaged across an area of land, a VIS\ngreater than 15 results.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":24},{"label":"99","code":null,"number":"99","title":"","text":"It would also appear to be the case that field sheets were not always shared, and that\nBAM plots were not positioned in a manner consistent with the BAM Manual - notably\ncharges laid by both parties.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"100","code":null,"number":"100","title":"","text":"In my view, Ms Black’s evidence as to the rationale for the location and the veracity of\nthe outputs recorded in the BAM plots is preferred and accepted over the doubts\nexpressed by Dr McLean. I accept, as do the experts, that BAM plot data that has been\ncollected over a period of around five years, dating from 2019 to 2024, is subject to\nvariability. As the subject of BAM plots is intrinsically natural, it follows that the data\ncollected by a BAM plot is also subject to the natural variability of climate and seasons,\nas well as by the intensity of grazing and other land management practices employed in\nthe maintenance of the land.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"101","code":null,"number":"101","title":"","text":"BAM plots are also recorded for the Services Corridor.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"102","code":null,"number":"102","title":"","text":"Dr McLean’s evidence is that the Services BDAR identifies vegetation as exotic and\nmay well record a low VIS, but even so credits may accrue, such as for the Southern\nMyotis. (Exhibit 12, pp 25-26)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"103","code":null,"number":"103","title":"","text":"Ms Black’s evidence is that five PCTs are identified, as are habitat uses within the road\nreserve and ecosystem credits have been applied. By way of illustration, Ms Black cites\nthe Scrub Turpentine that has been identified and impacts wholly avoided.\nRhodemnia rubescens","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"104","code":null,"number":"104","title":"","text":"It is commonly held by the experts that Rhodemnia rubescens, otherwise known as\nScrub Turpentine, is a critically endangered species, largely because of a fungal\ndisease known as Puccinia psidii, or Myrtle Rust.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"105","code":null,"number":"105","title":"","text":"As such, the Scrub Turpentine is a species requiring consideration of whether serious\nand irreversible impacts of a kind described at cl 6.7 of the Biodiversity Conservation\nRegulation 2017 (BC Regulation).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"106","code":null,"number":"106","title":"","text":"A total of 340 individuals stand on the site, including 300 that are located within the\nStewardship Site. Stands, or patches, of Scrub Turpentine are depicted on Figure 29 of\nthe BDAR, including seven individuals that are impacted by the proposal.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"107","code":null,"number":"107","title":"","text":"The proposal includes the translocation of 33 Scrub Turpentine to the BMP Lands, and\na trial program for treatment of the myrtle rust.\nBiconvex paperbark","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"108","code":null,"number":"108","title":"The Subdivision BDAR records the survey and mapping of Biconvex Paperbark","text":"equating to around 1.29 hectares within the development footprint on the site. A further\n12.41 hectares is mapped within the BSA land, and an additional area of 0.54 hectares\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 25/80\nis mapped within the 10m buffer and the BMP land.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":25},{"label":"109","code":null,"number":"109","title":"","text":"Stands, or patches, of Biconvex Paperbark on site are depicted on Figure 27 of the\nBDAR.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"110","code":null,"number":"110","title":"The Scientific Committee, established under the Threatened Species Conservation Act","text":"1995, has found that Biconvex Paperbark, determined to be a vulnerable species,\noccurs in coastal area of NSW from Jervis Bay to Port Macquarie, and in concentrated\npopulations in the Gosford/Wyong area, where most occur on private land or in the\nroad reserve. Due to the propensity to grow in multiple stems from single rootstock, an\nestimate of population size is not possible from visual inspection alone (Exhibit 14).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"111","code":null,"number":"111","title":"","text":"More distant from the site, the BDAR notes 415 records of the Biconvex Paperbark\nwithin a 10km range of the site over the last 20 years, including some records within\nclose proximity to the site.\nMagenta Lilly Pilly","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"112","code":null,"number":"112","title":"Eight individual Magenta Lilly Pilly are identified within the site proposed for","text":"development. Figure 30 of the Subdivision BDAR shows three individuals are directly\nimpacted and are proposed for translocation to the BMP Lands. Three are retained with\nencroachment resulting in credits being accrued, and two are located within the\nStewardship Site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"113","code":null,"number":"113","title":"","text":"More distant from the site, thirty-two records of the Magenta Lilly Pilly are recorded\nwithin 10km of the site, according to the BDAR over the last 20 years.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"114","code":null,"number":"114","title":"","text":"It is relevant to record here that an initial survey undertaken by Travers Bushfire and\nEcology in 2020 recorded a greater number of 60 individuals on the site. The BDAR\nexplains that follow up surveys determined the species was incorrectly identified, and\nwhere relevant, distinguished from two other species.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"115","code":null,"number":"115","title":"","text":"The ecology and aboricultural experts agree that it is likely all individual Magenta Lilly\nPilly have been detected within the vicinity of the vehicle crossing over Deep Creek, but\nthat it is appropriate to increase credits associated with the species to factor in\nappropriate buffer distances proposed by Dr McLean.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"116","code":null,"number":"116","title":"","text":"Magenta Lilly Pilly is also vulnerable to Myrtle Rust. However, unlike Rhodemnia, or\nScrub Turpentine, Magenta Lilly Pilly is cultivated in eastern Australia as an ornamental\ngarden plant. (Exhibit Q, p13)\nSurveys of fauna are incomplete","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"117","code":null,"number":"117","title":"","text":"The ecology experts are apart on the adequacy of surveys undertaken on site in\nrespect of fauna.\nAdequacy of owl surveys","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"118","code":null,"number":"118","title":"","text":"There is no dispute between the relevant experts that surveys have been undertaken\nfor three kinds of large forest owls; the Powerful Owl, the Sooty Owl and the Masked\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 26/80\nOwl.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":26},{"label":"119","code":null,"number":"119","title":"","text":"However, Dr McLean is of the view that the surveys undertaken are inadequate, such\nthat the impacts likely from the development are not correctly assessed nor properly\noffset.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"120","code":null,"number":"120","title":"","text":"On the basis of the methods agreed between the parties to apply, Dr Mclean’s written\nevidence is that adequate surveys for nest trees must be completed, and where such\ntrees are found, a buffer of 100m must be provided or species credits retired.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"121","code":null,"number":"121","title":"","text":"Dr McLean’s oral evidence is that nesting trees are distinguished by openings of\n200mm in diameter, a discoloration known as ‘whitewash’, as well as the remains of\nmammals that form part of the diet for the Powerful Owl. Furthermore, hollow bearing\ntrees that have died, known as ‘stags’ are also distinct and easily identified.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"122","code":null,"number":"122","title":"","text":"Monitoring a ‘stag tree’ is known as stag watching - a process that is generally timed to\ncoincide with dusk when the Powerful Owl is likely to be active.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"123","code":null,"number":"123","title":"","text":"Ms Black notes the surveys, undertaken by both Travers Ecology and AEP, are those\nrecorded on Table 14 of the BDAR (Exhibit M, folios 90-91) and include:\n(1) Hollow bearing tree and Habitat assessment on 1 August 2019, and on 14-16\nDecember 2020.\n(2) Spotlighting, Stagwatching and Call Playback on 25-26 June 2019, 01 August\n2019 and on 2-3 September 2019\n(3) Songmeter, on 25 June 2018 to 14 July 2018","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"124","code":null,"number":"124","title":"","text":"Table 14 also records the survey period identified by the BAM-C as May - August.\nSurveys in September and December clearly fall outside this period, a fact\nacknowledged by Ms Black. However, none of the habitat assessment methods\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 27/80\nidentified the presence of a nesting tree or roosting tree for Powerful Owl, Sooty Owl, or\nMasked Owl in either the development site, or the C2 zoned land. As such, no polygons\ncentred on a nesting or roosting tree have been generated.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":27},{"label":"125","code":null,"number":"125","title":"","text":"Dr McLean’s evidence is that just as the Glossy Black Cockatoo has been identified to\nbe potentially breeding in the vicinity of the site and credits retired accordingly, there is\nlikewise potential for the Sooty Owl and Powerful Owl to be breeding in the vicinity of\nthe site, and credits deserve to be retired also.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"126","code":null,"number":"126","title":"","text":"Ms Black’s evidence is that the identification of a Powerful Owl in the call-playback by\nTravers accords with AEP data, but at no time did Travers or AEP identify a nesting or\nroosting tree.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"127","code":null,"number":"127","title":"","text":"To the extent the Travers assessment relies on work undertaken by Mr John Young, a\nnoted naturalist, Dr McLean uses his own experience of participating in a study known\nas ‘Birds in Backyard’ to question whether the stag watching conducted by Mr Young on\n2-3 September 2019 was unsuccessful because Owl chicks left the nest prior to Mr\nYoung’s study.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"128","code":null,"number":"128","title":"","text":"Dr McLean has reviewed data derived from the ‘Birds in Backyard’ project to suggest\nthere is an equal chance that chicks leave the nest from 16 August to 16 September,\nand the timing of a survey such as that conducted by Mr Young should be guided by\nspecialist expertise in addition to the timeframe suggested by the BAM-C.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"129","code":null,"number":"129","title":"","text":"Separately, the breeding behaviour of the Sooty Owl and Marked Owl is less\npredictable, so surveys made at only a single point during the year on 2-3 September is\nalso inadequate, according to Dr McLean. Instead, surveys across several months are\nadvised.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"130","code":null,"number":"130","title":"","text":"Given such an absence of knowledge about the potential location of trees used by the\nlarge forest owls for breeding purposes, Dr McLean concludes proper consideration\nhas not been given to avoidance and minimisation of impacts and a buffer of 100m\napplied to any such trees.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"131","code":null,"number":"131","title":"","text":"However, as I understand it, the conclusion of the surveys undertaken is that trees to\nwhich such a buffer could be applied have not been identified. As such, there is not an\nabsence of knowledge so much as what might be described as knowledge as to the\nabsence of nesting and roosting trees.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 28/80\nAdequacy of Squirrel Glider surveys","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":28},{"label":"132","code":null,"number":"132","title":"Similarly, Dr McLean maintains that the surveys undertaken fail to demonstrate the","text":"Squirrel Glider is not present on the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"133","code":null,"number":"133","title":"","text":"To the extent the surveys cited in the BDAR are consistent with the Central Coast\nCouncil Flora and Fauna Guidelines (2019) (Council Guidelines), those guidelines, of\nwhich Dr McLean is an author, are not suited to the scale and complexity of the subject\nsite.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"134","code":null,"number":"134","title":"Ms Black’s written evidence is that absent more relevant guidelines, the Council","text":"Guidelines were used as a base, with surveys involving detailed camera trapping by 55\ncameras deployed across the site for 4 weeks between late December 2020 to late\nJanuary 2021, comprising a total of 1746 trap nights. Cameras were set both at a\nheight greater than 3m, and of around 500mm, with at least 4 cameras in the first\nhectare of suitable habitat followed thereafter by 2 additional cameras for each hectare.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"135","code":null,"number":"135","title":"","text":"Such a duration and intensity exceeds the minimum of two weeks recommended in the\nCouncil Guidelines. Additionally, nocturnal surveys were conducted across 14 nights\nand songmeters deployed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"136","code":null,"number":"136","title":"","text":"Ms Black also describes the preparation of baits consisting of rolled oats, peanut butter\nand honey designed to lure a Squirrel Glider.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"137","code":null,"number":"137","title":"","text":"Ms Black agrees the site is suited to the habitat of the Squirrel Glider, but none were\nfound.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"138","code":null,"number":"138","title":"","text":"Dr McLean’s own experience is that radio tracking yields superior results to remote\ncameras, recording or spotlighting, and cites research not provided to the Court as to\nthe probability of detection from such cameras at either 14% or 16%.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"139","code":null,"number":"139","title":"","text":"Additionally, the cameras that were deployed on the site were not located in the vicinity\nof winter flowering trees and rely on camera trigger times ranging from around 0.2\nsecond to 1 second, with a shutter speed of around 0.5-0.6 seconds. Such speeds rely\non bait to maintain the interest of the animal for long enough to record.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"140","code":null,"number":"140","title":"","text":"I note here the experts agree that the Services BDAR included outputs from camera\ntraps and spotlights to which Dr Mclean’s evidence at [138] also applies. In addition, Dr\nMclean’s experience is that even when radiotracking is used, Dr McLean could only\nobserve an animal on around 30% of occasions. Such is the difficulty involved in survey\nwork.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"141","code":null,"number":"141","title":"","text":"Ms Black cites references taken from the BDARs in which the type of survey, and date\non which the surveys were undertaken (Exhibit 12, p 42). These surveys include the\nuse of cameras, visual habitat assessment during daylight and nocturnal periods, use\nof songmeter, call playback and the like. The methods and frequency of such survey\nand assessment exceeds requirements according to Ms Black.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 29/80\nLittle Lorikeet and Gang Gang Cockatoo","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":29},{"label":"142","code":null,"number":"142","title":"","text":"Dr McLean extends his criticism to the effort made to assess the likely impact of the\nproposal to the habitat of the Gang Gang Cockatoo and Little Lorikeet.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"143","code":null,"number":"143","title":"","text":"In particular, surveys were not undertaken in Spring when breeding is likely to be\ndetected, and the identification of hollow-bearing trees does not include consideration\nof use by such fauna.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"144","code":null,"number":"144","title":"","text":"Ms Black considers it more accurate to state the period for surveys of such species is\nOctober to January - a period in which the surveys were undertaken. Furthermore, the\nGang Gang Cockatoo is distinctive and loud so it is difficult to miss, and again the\nnumber of surveys exceeds the number required.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"145","code":null,"number":"145","title":"","text":"The Little Lorikeet is recorded in the area over the last two decade, and while no\nevidence of breeding was found, foraging was noted and so ecosystem credits have\nbeen applied.\nFindings on adequacy of surveys","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"146","code":null,"number":"146","title":"","text":"As stated at [94], I consider that material within the BDARs that is derived from use of\nthe BAM-C software to be sufficiently independent and data-driven.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"147","code":null,"number":"147","title":"","text":"For the reasons set out at [100], I find the BAM plots, and surveys undertaken in\nrespect of flora and fauna have been prepared with appropriate diligence, are well\ndocumented and, in my view, are able to be adequately defended against the\nassertions of Dr McLean.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"148","code":null,"number":"148","title":"","text":"BAM plot data that has been collected over a period of around five years can be\nreasonably expected to vary in its outputs, given variation in climatic conditions, density\nof grazing stock and other land management methods commonly deployed in farm\nactivities.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"149","code":null,"number":"149","title":"","text":"I accept Ms Black’s evidence that the number and type of assessment and surveys\nundertaken are sufficient, and may exceed requirements, and that attention was paid to\nensure a variety of survey types and technologies have been used to provide every\nopportunity for fauna to be recorded in the subject site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"150","code":null,"number":"150","title":"","text":"This included reliance on BAM plots and not on rapid data points alone. Ms Black’s oral\nevidence included a description of how BAM plots completed in the services corridor\nwere tailored to the constrained geometry of the road reserve. Nowhere in oral\nevidence was Dr McLean able to identify the incorrect identification of a PCT.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"151","code":null,"number":"151","title":"","text":"The standard for Stevens to achieve is not Dr Mclean’s standard. Nor is it the standard\nDr McLean may have derived from the thesis or studies by others. It is instead whether\nthe survey methods adopted are reasonable, absent agreed or published guidelines on\nthe subject. Those guidelines authored by Dr Mclean cited at [133], when followed and\nsupplemented with additional monitoring, are considered by Dr Mclean as inadequate\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 30/80\nor inappropriate. Where models relied on by Stevens vary from those developed by Dr\nMclean, Dr Mclean is suspect, for reasons that are, at times, speculative or vary from\nhis own adopted practices.\nImpacts of proposed fill","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":30},{"label":"152","code":null,"number":"152","title":"","text":"As the extent of earthworks proposed on the site and the diversion of the watercourse,\nand potential impacts of it on the biodiversity values of the site are at issue, it is\nnecessary to consider those matters within the gamut of whether impacts are\nadequately avoided or minimised in the manner proposed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"153","code":null,"number":"153","title":"","text":"Stevens submits that the proposed earthworks and works proposed to Creek B are\ninterrelated and essential to achieving development of a kind sought in the WDCP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"154","code":null,"number":"154","title":"","text":"Chapter 6.25 of the WDCP deals with Rural Residential Development on certain land\nwithin Mardi (Exhibit 2, folios 673-683), including the subject site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"155","code":null,"number":"155","title":"","text":"One purpose of the WDCP is to set out requirements for subdivision and development\nfor the site following the rezoning of the land for residential development described at\n[29].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"156","code":null,"number":"156","title":"","text":"The objectives of the DCP are:\n“• To provide guidance for the orderly subdivision and development of the site.\n• To appropriately integrate development with the existing built and natural environment.\n• To enable development of the land to proceed in a manner that is sensitive to theenvironmental characteristics of the area.\n• To ensure that the land is adequately serviced.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"157","code":null,"number":"157","title":"","text":"An objective of Section 2.1 Subdivision Design is “to ensure that any future subdivision\nof the site is sympathetic to site constraints.” The Council contends the subdivision is\nnot sympathetic to the site constraints given the extensive earthworks proposed which\nhave resulted in significant vegetation removal and environmental impacts created by\nthe road location necessitating the realignment of the creek and proposing culvert\ncrossings instead of bridge crossings to access the Stage 5 lots.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"158","code":null,"number":"158","title":"","text":"The proposal seeks to alter site levels by the importation of fill to achieve levels of up to\n5m above those evident on the site today. A total of 256,629m of material is proposed\nto be imported on to the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"159","code":null,"number":"159","title":"","text":"Stevens submits the proposed subdivision and the associated fill is a response to the\nlow-lying land on the site and the requirements at Chapter 6.25, Section 2.5(e) of the\nWDCP for a development proposal on the site to include alterations to existing\n3\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 31/80\nwatercourse, and the provision of riparian zones including, at a minimum, one riparian\ncorridor within the central portion of the R5 zone of the subject site to be retained under\nthe ownership of a Community Association.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":31},{"label":"160","code":null,"number":"160","title":"","text":"Experts in flooding, engineering and ecology conferred in the preparation of a joint\nexpert report dealing with the inter-related issues between the disciplines (Exhibit 6)\nand reach substantial agreement.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"161","code":null,"number":"161","title":"","text":"In respect of fill, the experts agree that the engineering design has generally sought to\nminimise the volume of imported fill to achieve the flood planning requirements and\ndrainage grades. (Exhibit 6, par 70)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"162","code":null,"number":"162","title":"","text":"The Council also relies on the objectives and requirements at Chapter 6.25, Section 2.4\nof the WDCP which deals with biodiversity protection management.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"163","code":null,"number":"163","title":"","text":"There are two primary issues that arise from the extent of fill proposed on the site:\n(1) Visual impact of the proposed development when viewed from Old Mardi Road,\nand\n(2) Watercourses are proposed to be diverted.\nVisual Impact","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"164","code":null,"number":"164","title":"","text":"The Council contends that the proposed development should be refused as the degree\nof earthworks described at [158] is excessive and results in a development inconsistent\nwith a rural setting.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"165","code":null,"number":"165","title":"","text":"In consideration of the planning contentions, of which visual impact is a part, the Court\nwas assisted by experts in town planning and visual impact assessment including:\nMr Dan Brindle, Town Planner, on behalf of the Applicant\nMr Ben Shoo, Visual Impact assessment, on behalf of the Applicant\nMr Gerard Turrisi, Town Planner, on behalf of the Council","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"166","code":null,"number":"166","title":"","text":"The experts conferred in the preparation of a joint expert report (Exhibit 3), and in a\nsupplementary joint expert report, directed at the completion of the on site view so as to\nconsider the evidence of Mr McMonnies, bushfire expert on behalf of the Applicant who\nparticipated in the conclave so as to consider the inter-relationship between bushfire\nbehaviour and the landscape proposed.\nLandscape design to Lots 7-17","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"167","code":null,"number":"167","title":"","text":"In his oral evidence, Mr Shoo explained that the photomontage images (Exhibit A, Tab\nY) had been prepared in consultation with Stevens’ landscape expert to ensure the\nimagery faithfully depicted the landscape planting at 5 year and 10 year maturity.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"168","code":null,"number":"168","title":"","text":"In particular, the experts considered the view looking north west when travelling north\nalong Old Maitland Road, in the direction of the proposed lots at Lots 7-17.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"169","code":null,"number":"169","title":"","text":"I note here that while the Lots encompass all lots between Lot 7 and 17, Lot 13 does\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 32/80\nnot front Old Maitland Road. Rather, Lot 13 addresses an internal road.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":32},{"label":"170","code":null,"number":"170","title":"","text":"Lot 17 is proposed for the purpose of a flood evacuation centre to be managed by the\nproposed Community Association in accordance with a flood evacuation management\nplan.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"171","code":null,"number":"171","title":"The planting arrangement and species selection shown on Landscape drawings L305","text":"(Exhibit A, Tab Y) consists of an 8m landscape buffer of open grass and a 4m strip of\nlow shrubs and ground cover. Together, these areas respond appropriately to the 12m\nwide easement along the frontage of the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"172","code":null,"number":"172","title":"","text":"Beyond this landscape treatment, a 5m strip of canopy trees provide screening at an\nupper level.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"173","code":null,"number":"173","title":"","text":"A post and rail fence is proposed to the frontage with Old Maitland Road, that Mr Shoo\nconsiders consistent with a rural character, beyond which no additional fencing is\nproposed that would obstruct a sightline.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"174","code":null,"number":"174","title":"","text":"As this landscape treatment presents to Old Maitland Road, the Council submits that a\ncohesive management approach to the frontage would ensure consistency. Such an\noutcome can only be achieved if the landscape treatment is managed by the\nCommunity Association, while ownership remains with the lot owner.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"175","code":null,"number":"175","title":"","text":"A condition to this effect, or restriction on title, is largely supported by the experts.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"176","code":null,"number":"176","title":"","text":"Guidelines prepared by Mr Shoo (Exhibit A, Tab Y) identify building envelope zones,\nsetbacks and landscape treatment around dwelling footprints.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"177","code":null,"number":"177","title":"","text":"The experts agree that no landscape is shown within Asset Protection Zones (APZ). Mr\nMcMonnies states that the landscaping to Lots 7-17 is wholly outside the APZ and,\nwhile this is seemingly at odds with the Bush Fire Safety Authority (BFSA) issued by the\nNSW RFS, that otherwise required the whole of the site to be considered an Inner\nProtection Zone, the Court may exercise the functions and discretions of the NSW RFS\nand vary any terms contained within the BFSA (Exhibit A, Tab 29).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"178","code":null,"number":"178","title":"","text":"On the basis of a proposed condition of consent that requires the landscape strip to be\nmanaged by the Community Association, I do not otherwise find the visual impact likely\nto result from the fill on the land, or the nature of development proposed, to be out of\ncharacter with the area when the nature and degree of landscape treatment is properly\nunderstood, along with the natural undulation of topography in the area.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 33/80\nThe order of the watercourses","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":33},{"label":"179","code":null,"number":"179","title":"","text":"The two watercourses on the site described at [28] give rise to contentions addressed\nby the evidence of water engineering experts Ms Louise Collier, on behalf of the\nCouncil, and Mr Chris Thomas, and Mr Chris Piper on behalf of Stevens. Mr Angus\nBrien also conferred on behalf of Stevens in respect of the site survey on which the\nwatercourse engineers relied.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":34},{"label":"180","code":null,"number":"180","title":"","text":"The experts conferred in the preparation of a joint expert report (initial joint report)\n(Exhibit 6), along with the experts in ecology, that resulted in the majority of contentions\nbeing resolved by agreement or agreed to be capable of resolution by conditions of\nconsent.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":34},{"label":"181","code":null,"number":"181","title":"Those matters remaining were the subject of supplementary joint expert reporting","text":"(Exhibit 8), that also considered supplementary survey information prepared by Mr\nBrien’s firm, ADW Johnson.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":34},{"label":"182","code":null,"number":"182","title":"","text":"A primary focus of the dispute between the water engineering experts lies in the\nclassification of Creek B.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":34},{"label":"183","code":null,"number":"183","title":"","text":"It is helpful at this point to describe various agreed features of Creek B as it traverses\nthe site from its entry point in the south west of the site, to its eventual exit in the north\neast of the site.\n(1) Dam 1 is located to the west of the site, on higher country.\n(2) The land falls gently from Dam 1 to Dam 2 across largely open grassland.\n(3) A channel of some sort runs between Dam 2 and the confluence of reach 1, 2\nand 3, (the confluence).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":34},{"label":"184","code":null,"number":"184","title":"","text":"The three reaches cited above are defined by the experts in Figure 2 of the initial joint\nreport (p 32), re-produced below:\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 34/80\nFigure 4: Overlay of the three reaches and confluence point on the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":34},{"label":"185","code":null,"number":"185","title":"","text":"The experts agree that Reach 1 is a second order watercourse, and that Reach 3 is a\nfirst order watercourse, however the experts are divided as to the characteristics of\nReach 2 and distil the scope of their disagreement in the following terms, adopting\nthose features at Figure A1 below:\n(1) Ms Collier believes two upstream inflows are evident in to Dam 1 towards the\nupper extent of Reach 2 that should be considered a second order watercourse.\n(2) Neither Mr Thomas or Mr Brien can identify a defined watercourse extending\nupstream from Dam 1, and so consider the watercourse between Dam 1 and\nDam 2 to be properly characterised as a first order watercourse, with\nwatercourse features becoming evident in the vicinity of the location marked\n‘P2A’.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 35/80\nFigure 5: Watercourse features superimposed on to the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":35},{"label":"186","code":null,"number":"186","title":"","text":"As it is put by Ms Collier, the behaviour of water on the site can be said, at its most\nbasic, to include a steep area of land that conveys water down its slopes to a flat area\nthat feed into a narrow portion of land.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":36},{"label":"187","code":null,"number":"187","title":"","text":"Such behaviour is evident from survey data, contours and LiDAR information.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":36},{"label":"188","code":null,"number":"188","title":"","text":"Ms Colliers explains her understanding of the terrain in the vicinity of Creek B in\nAnnexure E of the initial joint expert report, summarised as follows:\n(1) The Controlled activities - Guidelines for riparian corridors on waterfront land,\npublished by the Department of Planning and Environment (Exhibit 6, Annexure\nD) relies on the Strahler stream order that, when applied to the site, shows\nstream order 1 and 2 present on the site.\n(2) Inspection of the site indicated features that varied from those on topographic\nand digital hydroline spatial databases. Assisted by inputs such as aerial\nimagery, surveys, field inspection, photographs and measurements, and the\nStrahler analysis tool in QGIS, Ms Collier located features consistent with\nwatercourses in the vicinity of Dam 1, and an ephemeral channel between Dam\n1 and Dam 2.\n(3) The results of the QGIS tool, depicted in a number of figures within Annexure E,\nshows what Ms Collier considers first order streams that are not mapped as\nhydro lines, but are depicted by her as pink dashed lines, where water flows can\nbe expected, but which vary from that depicted in the assessment undertaken\nby AEP of the Aquatic Ecology (BDAR, Exhibit M, Appendix J, Figure 6).\n(4) Downslope of Dam 3, Ms Collier believes a watercourse, albeit discontinuous,\nextends towards the Confluence, although is not joined by the hydro line\notherwise shown intersecting with it in Ms Collier’s Figure E16.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":36},{"label":"189","code":null,"number":"189","title":"","text":"Mr Thomas’ evidence is that while the topography above Dam 1 is steep and serves to\nconvey water to Dam 1, the topography is not consistent with the features of a\nwatercourse when the detailed survey prepared by ADW Johnson in July 2024 (July\nSurvey) (Exhibit 8, Attachment A) is matched to field observations.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":36},{"label":"190","code":null,"number":"190","title":"","text":"Instead, the July Survey suggests the land form in the vicinity of Dam 1 includes a side-\nflow spillway from the south-western corner of the dam wall, and a channel that extends\na distance of about 30m to the south west of the dam, consistent with the spillway.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 36/80\nSuch a feature is likely to have been dug by a landowner for such a purpose, and to\nalso receive water from upslope as a ‘funnel’, but cannot be properly described as a\nnatural watercourse.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":36},{"label":"191","code":null,"number":"191","title":"","text":"Such observations include the lack of a bed or embankment expected of a\nwatercourse, obviously lacking in Figure 7 and 8 of the supplementary joint report,\naccording to Mr Thomas, and the accumulation of leaf litter that appears to have been\ntransported and deposited by sheet flow, and not a stream.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"192","code":null,"number":"192","title":"","text":"In contrast, Ms Collier provides marked images at Figure 3 and 4 of the supplementary\njoint report that indicate linear depressions or channels upslope of Dam 1.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"193","code":null,"number":"193","title":"","text":"According to Mr Thomas, the channels identified by Ms Collier are most likely desire\nlines formed by cattle or other forms of depressions of a kind seen commonly on the\nland, that may have their origin in historical tree removal, and which hold standing\nwater due to the underlying clay in the soil substructure.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"194","code":null,"number":"194","title":"","text":"I state here that I agree with the observations made by Mr Thomas. I accept that the\nJuly survey is likely to be a more robust and ‘ground-truthed’ data set than the GIS-\nbased tool, and which additionally accords, in my assessment, with observations made\nduring the onsite view.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"195","code":null,"number":"195","title":"","text":"The experts are also apart on how the area between Dam 1 and Dam 2 is properly\ncharacterised.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"196","code":null,"number":"196","title":"According to Ms Collier, the July Survey validates her observations and opinions","text":"expressed in Annexure E of the initial joint report, and summarised at [188], and is\nconsistent with the QGIS mapping prepared by her at Figure E10 of Annexure E.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"197","code":null,"number":"197","title":"Mr Thomas agrees that watercourse features become evident between Dam 1 and","text":"Dam 2, at the point marked P2A, and extend to the Confluence with Reach 3. However,\nMr Thomas is also of the view that the depression is characteristic of a drainage\nchannel dug by backhoe, as indicated by the mounds positioned to one side that are\nnot otherwise seen in the landscape.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"198","code":null,"number":"198","title":"","text":"I state here that I agree with Mr Thomas. I accept the proportions and almost vertical\nsides of the channel appear consistent with excavation by a narrow backhoe, and does\nnot appear to be a natural channel or even a natural channel that has been artificially\nimproved. As such, the drainage channel at this point does not answer the description\nof a river, and so the land is not waterfront land by virtue of this drainage channel.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"199","code":null,"number":"199","title":"","text":"The next area of contest between the experts is an area that spans from the ridgeline to\nthe south above Dam 3 to the idea identified in Figure 5 above as the ‘confluence’.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"200","code":null,"number":"200","title":"","text":"According to Ms Collier, a form of watercourse is evident from the ridge to the south of\nDam 3. It is agreed by the experts that there is a watercourse between the locations\nidentified as P3A and P3B, and is also evidenced by the large area of “boggy mess”\nbetween P3A and the area identified as the ‘confluence’.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"201","code":null,"number":"201","title":"The Council relies on the Geotechnical Investigation prepared on behalf of the","text":"Applicant by Martens and Associates (Geotechnical Report) (Exhibit A, Tab 17, Figure\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 37/80\n2) that likewise depicts graphical indicators of a watercourse above Dam 3.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":37},{"label":"202","code":null,"number":"202","title":"","text":"A topographical map prepared by Ms Collier using QGIS (Exhibit 8, Figure 2) shows a\nsimilar watercourse pattern to that depicted on Figure 3 of the BDAR, on which\nwatercourses and hydro lines are overlaid.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"203","code":null,"number":"203","title":"","text":"Mr Thomas agrees the area between P3A and P3B below Dam 3 is a watercourse, but\nis not within a defined channel and does not connect to Creek B at the confluence.\nRather, water is conveyed overland between Dam 3 and the area between P3A and\nP3B, and is then conveyed by sheet flow to the confluence with Creek B. This explains\nthe “boggy mess” upslope of the confluence.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"204","code":null,"number":"204","title":"","text":"As for the Geotechnical Report, Mr Thomas considers figures prepared primarily for the\npurpose of depicting the location of boreholes drilled on the site should not be relied on\nas an accurate survey of watercourses.\nThe watercourse is proposed to be diverted","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"205","code":null,"number":"205","title":"","text":"As stated at [28], there are two waterways on the site. Stevens proposes the diversion\nand realignment of one of the two waterways. It is helpful to describe the current state\nof those waterways, and the works proposed in their vicinity.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"206","code":null,"number":"206","title":"","text":"Firstly, Deep Creek traverses the north west of the site and effectively demarcates the\ndevelopment identified as Stage 4, from that identified as Stage 5.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"207","code":null,"number":"207","title":"","text":"Two bridges to convey vehicles are proposed over Deep Creek. Local reshaping of the\nwaterway to achieve flow conveyance and scour protection is also proposed, according\nto Engineering drawings prepared by Northrop (Engineering Plans) (Exhibit A, Tab A3,\nDA3.C02.11).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"208","code":null,"number":"208","title":"","text":"Localised tree removal is also proposed. In the joint expert report of the ecologists, Mr\nMuir recommends a methodology to protect trees within the vicinity of the scour\nprotection works proposed around the two bridges (Exhibit 12, para 455).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"209","code":null,"number":"209","title":"","text":"Secondly, Creek B is proposed to be both diverted and piped for a section within the\nsite. Where Deep Creek is proposed to be spanned by bridge structures, access over\nCreek B is via box culverts.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"210","code":null,"number":"210","title":"","text":"The two watercourses are part of the strategy to dispose of stormwater from the site.\nThe Preliminary Engineering Design Report (Exhibit A, Tab 15, p14) explains that\nstormwater, in minor storm events, is to be conveyed via a piped drainage system to\nCreek B during Stages 1-4, while development in Stage 5 will discharge to Deep Creek.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"211","code":null,"number":"211","title":"","text":"Works are proposed in the vicinity of Creek B to construct what is referred to in the\nEngineering Plans as a ‘naturalised drainage channel’ that includes pools and riffles,\nmeanders, scour protection, vegetation and bank stabilisation.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"212","code":null,"number":"212","title":"","text":"The Council contends the proposed realignment and diversion of ‘Creek B’ on the site\nis unacceptable because of the impact it would have on biodiversity values of an\nexisting stream, erosion and bank stability. Further, such a diversion is inconsistent with\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 38/80\nthe Guidelines for riparian corridors on waterfront land, published by the Department of\nPlanning and Environment, 2022 (Exhibit 1 Tab 45) as such works are not permitted as\na Controlled Activity under the Water Management Act 2000.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":38},{"label":"213","code":null,"number":"213","title":"","text":"According to Ms Black, the brief provided to Northrop in preparing the Engineering\nPlans was for a swale design appropriate for a First order watercourse, except for an\narea identified as the confluence of waterways which was assessed as second order\nwatercourse. Any impacts to EEC’s are limited to severely and highly degraded\ncommunities, with areas assessed to be in good and moderate condition avoided within\nlands zoned C3, and where tree protection is proposed to be supervised by an AQF5\nlevel arborist. (Exhibit 6, paras 158-160).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":39},{"label":"214","code":null,"number":"214","title":"","text":"To achieve the naturalised drainage channel, the Engineering Plans depict an extent of\ncut and fill (Exhibit A, Tab A3, C03.01) that Mr Piper considers consistent with Ms\nBlack’s brief to achieve a low flow channel and overbank with pools and riffles. By\nexcavating the land in the location of the naturalised drainage channel, Mr Piper’s\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 39/80\nevidence is that the extent of fill in those areas identified for development in Stage 3\nand 4 is minimised by around 1-2m below that otherwise required if excavation did not\noccur.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":39},{"label":"215","code":null,"number":"215","title":"","text":"Mr Thomas and Mr Piper believe that a ‘do nothing’ approach risks erosion and\ndestabilisation in Reach 1 and 2 of a kind agreed between the experts in the initial joint\nreport (para 50).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":40},{"label":"216","code":null,"number":"216","title":"","text":"Should filling occur on the site with the channel in its current state, stormwater\nbehaviour is likely to be concentrated in higher velocities, further eroding the\nwatercourse path. To install scour protection would necessitate a re-profiling of the\nchannel, the removal of some vegetation and topsoil to achieve a firm sub-base, and\nthe laying of geotextile fabric and the like.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":40},{"label":"217","code":null,"number":"217","title":"","text":"In light of these alternatives, Mr Thomas and Mr Piper consider the proposal to realign\nReach 2 and 3 to be preferable and, as those Reaches are properly understood to be\nfirst order watercourses, the proposal to do so is permitted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":40},{"label":"218","code":null,"number":"218","title":"","text":"It appears relevant to once again state that the experts agree that design features such\nas the pool and riffle sequence and scour protection details are sufficient measures to\ndemonstrate a level of protection against stream erosion, and that the velocity\ncomparison plots at Annexure B of the initial joint report shows the proposal does not\nresult in an adverse impact on velocities downstream.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":40},{"label":"219","code":null,"number":"219","title":"","text":"As Reach 1 is agreed to be a second order watercourse, Mr Thomas and Mr Piper\ndefer to the assessment of others as to the ecological impact of what they describe as\nthe reshaping and rehabilitation of that portion of the watercourse.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":40},{"label":"220","code":null,"number":"220","title":"","text":"It is Dr McLeans evidence that the width of the corridor in which the naturalised\ndrainage channel is proposed is insufficient. The corridor is not specified as a zone in\nthe BMP and nowhere does the documentation outline landscape treatments such as\ntree retention or tree planting, or other forms of vegetation that would support aquatic\nvegetation.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":40},{"label":"221","code":null,"number":"221","title":"","text":"For instance, the Arboricultural Impact Assessment (Exhibit M, Tab 3) has not assessed\ntrees in the vicinity of the proposed naturalised channel to determine the type of\nvegetation likely to be retained in a 20m wide corridor, required by a first order\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 40/80\nwatercourse, compared to a 40m wide corridor required for a second order\nwatercourse. As such, the proposed naturalised drainage channel provides no benefit\nto biodiversity.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":40},{"label":"222","code":null,"number":"222","title":"","text":"Dr McLean also considers the proposed naturalised channel to have a high edge-to-\narea ratio that should be mitigated by providing a greater vegetated riparian corridor to\nthe south of the proposed naturalised channel, that would allow fauna movement\nbetween areas zoned C2 and C3.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":41},{"label":"223","code":null,"number":"223","title":"","text":"Ms Black cites provisions at s 91 of the Water Management Act 2000 and the Fisheries\nManagement Act 1994 as the guide adopted by her in assessment of the proposed\nworks to Creek B.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":41},{"label":"224","code":null,"number":"224","title":"","text":"Ms Black’s evidence is that her assessment, within the area of Reach 2, concluded\nsignificant modification that suggests a purpose of draining the land, in a manner I\nunderstand to be similar to Mr Thomas’ observation at [197].\nFindings on the watercourses","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":41},{"label":"225","code":null,"number":"225","title":"","text":"A river is defined by the Water Management Act 2000 dictionary in the following terms:\nriver includes-\n(a) any watercourse, whether perennial or intermittent and whether comprising a naturalchannel or a natural channel artificially improved, and\n(b) any tributary, branch or other watercourse into or from which a watercourse referredto in paragraph (a) flows, and\n(c) anything declared by the regulations to be a river, whether or not it also forms part ofa lake or estuary, but does not include anything declared by the regulations not to be ariver.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":41},{"label":"226","code":null,"number":"226","title":"","text":"The definition of ‘waterfront land’ is also found in the Dictionary of the Water\nManagement Act in the following terms:\nwaterfront land means-\n(a) the bed of any river, together with any land lying between the bed of the river and aline drawn parallel to, and the prescribed distance inland of, the highest bank of theriver, or\n(a1) the bed of any lake, together with any land lying between the bed of the lake and aline drawn parallel to, and the prescribed distance inland of, the shore of the lake, or\n(a2) the bed of any estuary, together with any land lying between the bed of the estuaryand a line drawn parallel to, and the prescribed distance inland of, the mean high watermark of the estuary, or\n(b) if the regulations so provide, the bed of the coastal waters of the State, and any landlying between the shoreline of the coastal waters and a line drawn parallel to, and theprescribed distance inland of, the mean high water mark of the coastal waters, wherethe prescribed distance is 40 metres or (if the regulations prescribe a lesser distance,either generally or in relation to a particular location or class of locations) that lesserdistance. Land that falls into 2 or more of the categories referred to in paragraphs\n(a), (a1) and (a2) may be waterfront land by virtue of any of the paragraphs relevant tothat land.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":41},{"label":"227","code":null,"number":"227","title":"The Controlled activities - Guidelines for riparian corridors on waterfront land,","text":"published by the Department of Planning and Environment (Exhibit 6, Annexure D)\ncontains recommendations for riparian corridor widths, that vary according to the order\nof the watercourse as follows:\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 41/80\nWatercourse type VRZ width (each side\nof watercourse)\nTotal RC\nwidth\n1st order 10 metres 20 m +\nchannel\nwidth\n2nd order 20 metres 40 m +\nchannel\nwidth\n3rd order 30 metres 60 m +\nchannel\nwidth\n4th order and greater (includes estuaries,\nwetlands and any parts of rivers influenced\nby tidal waters)\nparts of rivers influenced by tidal waters)\n40 metres 80 m +\nchannel\nwidth\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 42/80\n“Note: where a watercourse does not exhibit the features of a defined channel with bedand banks, the department may determine that the watercourse is not waterfront landfor the purposes of the WM Act.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":41},{"label":"228","code":null,"number":"228","title":"","text":"The Subdivision BDAR describes Creek B as highly modified, with the portion located\nin the BMP Lands having limited watercourse features, such as those identified in the\nDepartment of Planning, Industry and Environment’s Natural Resources Access\nRegulator Waterfront Land Tool (May 2020) to include pools, riffles, meanders and\naquatic vegetation. (Exhibit M, folio 172)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"229","code":null,"number":"229","title":"","text":"The parties submit that it is not necessary for the Court to make a finding on the order\nof watercourses comprising Reaches 1-3. That said, I have found that Reach 2 is not\nfed by an upstream watercourse, and Reach 3 would appear discontinuous, devolving\ninto the ‘boggy mess’ before it connects to Reach 1.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"230","code":null,"number":"230","title":"","text":"The experts agree Reach 1 is a second order watercourse. According to the Guidelines\nfor Riparian Corridors, a 20m Vegetated Riparian Zone (VRZ) applies from the top of\nthe bank on each side of the watercourse. That said, certain works and activities are\npermitted on waterfront land, such as cycleways and paths, detention basins,\nstormwater outlet structures and essential services and road crossings of any sort.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"231","code":null,"number":"231","title":"","text":"The sections depicted in the Landscape Plans suggest a VRZ greater than 20m, with\ncertain works and activities proposed within this corridor. The Preliminary Engineering\nDesign Report (Exhibit A, Tab 15) likewise describes the VRZ, referred to as the central\ndrainage corridor, as a 20m Riparian Corridor to each side.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"232","code":null,"number":"232","title":"On 16 September 2021, the Natural Resources Access Regulator uploaded General","text":"terms of approval to the Planning Portal, noting those conditions set out do not\nconstitute an approval under the Water Management Act 2000. (Exhibit A, Tab 22)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"233","code":null,"number":"233","title":"Section 201 of the Fisheries Management Act 1994 (Fisheries Act) precludes dredging","text":"work within aquatic habitats except with the authority of a permit issued by the Minister.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"234","code":null,"number":"234","title":"","text":"On 24 August 2021, the Department of Primary Industries issued General Terms of\napproval (Exhibit M, Appendix P), including a requirement for Stevens to apply for a\npermit of a kind referenced above, and subject to review by the Department of Primary\nIndustries of the road crossings over Deep Creek.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"235","code":null,"number":"235","title":"","text":"Dr McLean’s concerns at [221] do not, to my understanding, appear to be borne out\nwhen the BDAR is carefully read. Trees within the area zoned C3 have in fact been\nassessed. The Subdivision BDAR includes a survey identifying 296 trees present, that\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 43/80\nwas subsequently relied on by a level 5 arborist to determine patches of trees and\nthose with High and Moderate retention value with the result being a proposal for 139\ntrees within the C3 zone to be removed. (Exhibit M, folios 154-155)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":43},{"label":"236","code":null,"number":"236","title":"Research cited by the Subdivision BDAR titled ‘Can the Regeneration of Vegetation","text":"from Riparian Seed Banks Support Biogeomorphic Succession and the Geomorphic\nRecovery of Degraded River Channels?’ is said to have found that even degraded\nfloodplains and riparian ecosystems retain seedbanks that can promote regeneration.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":44},{"label":"237","code":null,"number":"237","title":"","text":"The number of trees proposed to be retained in the C3 zoned land will continue to add\nto the seed bank deposits along the bank of the naturalised drainage channel over the\n20 years of management proposed by the BMP, and the relevant experts agree that a\nwatering regime will provide water to those trees retained in the C3 zone during\nconstruction works.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":44},{"label":"238","code":null,"number":"238","title":"Next, Dr McLeans’ assertion that proposed landscape treatments are lacking from the","text":"development application appears mistaken when the Landscape plans (Exhibit A, Tab\nA4) are read together, including the Indicative Creek Line plan (DA L214 Rev L),\nsections CC and DD (DA L216 Rev L), and the plant schedule applicable to detention\nbasins and swales (DA L304 Rev L).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":44},{"label":"239","code":null,"number":"239","title":"","text":"Furthermore, I note the Subdivision BDAR relies on principles from the Brisbane City\nCouncil, Natural Channel Design (2003) as the basis for proposed reinstatement of\nnatural features within the BMP lands, including aquatic species within the creek line to\ntoe of bank, and a mix of PCTs. (Exhibit M, folio 172)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":44},{"label":"240","code":null,"number":"240","title":"","text":"The proposal to realign a portion of Creek B where it answers the description of a first\norder watercourse is consistent with Table 2 of the Guidelines for Riparian Corridors\nthat permits such realignment. Next, when the engineering plans, landscape plans and\nSubdivision BDAR are read together with the provisions of the BMP, the VRZ appears\nof a width that is consistent with that part of the Guidelines for Riparian Corridors\napplicable to second order watercourses.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":44},{"label":"241","code":null,"number":"241","title":"","text":"To the extent the Council contends the proposed works to Creek B are inconsistent with\nthe objectives of the C2 and C3 zone at [33] and [34], the agreed position of the\nexperts at [161] that acceptable flood planning requirements and drainage grades have\nbeen achieved is indicative of reasonable effects being achieved on the ecological\nvalues of land in both zones.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 44/80\nAssessment of off-site impacts","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":44},{"label":"242","code":null,"number":"242","title":"","text":"In broad terms, there are two aspects of the proposal that are identified as likely\nimpacts of the development that do not arise as site-specific impacts, being impacts of\nthe proposed development on the development site, but as off-site impacts (Ballina\nShire Council v Palm Lake Works Pty Ltd [2020] NSWLEC 41, at [5])","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":45},{"label":"243","code":null,"number":"243","title":"","text":"These aspects are:\n(1) Firstly, the means by which the proposed development is serviced by utilities,\nproposed within the road reserve.\n(2) Secondly, impacts that are, in essence, construction-related impacts resulting\nfrom truck movements and the like that have the potential to adversely impact\nthe acoustic amenity of residents in existing dwellings along the route likely to\nbe taken by such trucks.\nServicing the proposed development","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":45},{"label":"244","code":null,"number":"244","title":"","text":"The area of land subject to the proposal within the services corridor is assessed by the\nServices BDAR as a total of 13.3 hectares.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":45},{"label":"245","code":null,"number":"245","title":"","text":"The further amended DA is supported by a letter prepared by AEP in respect of\nArboricultural impact on what is described as the ‘external work package’ dated 8\nMarch 2024 (AEP Offsite Assessment).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":45},{"label":"246","code":null,"number":"246","title":"","text":"The methodology adopted by the Arborists in the preparation of the AEP Offsite\nAssessment is described at Section 1 of the letter as a visual tree assessment to\nevaluate the health and condition, height and other dimensions of 16 trees.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":45},{"label":"247","code":null,"number":"247","title":"The Services BDAR states that early design investigations assessed the potential for","text":"alignment along the road reserve, or alternatively through private land. Ecological\ninvestigation determined vegetation on private lands was of greater biodiversity value.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 45/80\nAccordingly, further surveys and arboricultural assessment were focused on the road\nreserve, and, relevantly, impact on the biodiversity values on private land was avoided.\n(Exhibit M, Tab 2, folio 77)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":45},{"label":"248","code":null,"number":"248","title":"","text":"Two primary methods have then been deployed to avoid or minimise the impact on the\nbiodiversity values of the road reserve. These include, firstly, the choice of alignment\nthe services are proposed to take. Secondly, by underboring the services trench, with\nretrieval pits at certain intervals, the disturbance of root systems to trees in the road\nreserve is minimised.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"249","code":null,"number":"249","title":"","text":"The extent of impact is quantified in the Services BDAR, supported by aerial imagery\non which the alignment, and the impact is overlaid, and also by responses to those\nstrategies and actions sought by Section 7 of the BAM, at Table 11 of the Services\nBDAR. The resulting impact is otherwise summarised on p 77 in the following terms:\n(1) 0.1 ha of PCT 1568 is impacted, with no associated TEC impacted\n(2) 0.54 ha of PCT 1564 is impacted, with no associated TEC impacted\n(3) One hollow bearing tree.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"250","code":null,"number":"250","title":"","text":"The works proposed in the services corridor also include pruning to trees within the\nvicinity of the entry to the site from Old Maitland Road to provide a sightline for vehicles\nentering and exiting the site, and for vehicles travelling along Old Maitland Road.\nArboricultural advice prepared by AEP dated 12 September 2024 (Exhibit M, Tab 7)\nstates that 14 trees require pruning to achieve a sightline measuring a distance of\n225m to the north, and 240m to the south.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"251","code":null,"number":"251","title":"","text":"In summary, this work is focused on lower order branches and does not require or\npropose the removal of native vegetation (Exhibit M, Tab 7). Rather, weed species are\nproposed to be removed to improve the environmental values of the area.\nAcoustic impact on existing dwellings on Old Maitland Road","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"252","code":null,"number":"252","title":"","text":"The Council contends that the large volume of fill proposed to be delivered to the site\nrequires a construction traffic management plan that identifies the management of the\nlarge number of truck movements expected to be generated by the proposal.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"253","code":null,"number":"253","title":"A Traffic Impact Assessment prepared by Intersect Traffic dated August 2023 (Traffic","text":"Report) (Exhibit A, Tab Q) assesses the local road network and capacity and traffic\nvolumes expected by the extent of fill proposed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"254","code":null,"number":"254","title":"","text":"The Traffic Report concludes the proposed importation of fill is likely to generate 46\nvehicles per hour (vph) in the AM and PM peak periods, which is within the capacity of\nthe road network to support, including the intersection with the M1 Motorway, otherwise\nknown as the Tuggerah Interchange.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"255","code":null,"number":"255","title":"","text":"Table 2 (p 7) of the Traffic Report tabulates the heavy traffic likely to be generated by\nthe importation of fill on to the site, in the following terms:\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 46/80\nStage 1Stage 2Stage 3Stage 4Stage 5\nTotal truck numbers 2167 3333 10833 21667 3667\nFill period (weeks) 2 3 9 17 3\nTruck numbers per day197 202 219 232 222\nTruck numbers per hour20 20 22 23 22\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 47/80\nTruck trips per hour 39 40 44 46 44","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":46},{"label":"256","code":null,"number":"256","title":"","text":"As the increased heavy traffic movements on Old Maitland Road has the potential to\ncontribute to the deterioration of the road pavement, the Traffic Report recommends\nthat a condition of consent require the Stevens to submit a dilapidation report to the\nCouncil, and further require Stevens to undertake any remediation works to the road\npavement.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":48},{"label":"257","code":null,"number":"257","title":"","text":"In a supplementary joint expert report prepared by the town planning experts (Exhibit\n7), the experts agree that no acoustic or vibration assessment of the traffic volumes\nhave been prepared, that might impact four houses visible from Old Maitland Road.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":48},{"label":"258","code":null,"number":"258","title":"","text":"On day 3 of the hearing, the Applicant filed a single expert report prepared by Mr Glenn\nLeembruggen of Acoustic Directions dated the same day, 14 August 2024 (Acoustic\nReport) (Exhibit G).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":48},{"label":"259","code":null,"number":"259","title":"The Acoustic Report concludes, when corrected noise levels contained in a letter","text":"authored by Mr Leembruggen dated 15 August 2024 (Exhibit L), are adopted:\n(1) A sound exposure level of 85 dBA applies to a vehicle travelling at 80km/h,\nwhen measured from a distance of 8m that would, for reasons set out, result in\nan L level of 66 dBA when 46 truck deliveries are factored over the course of\nan hour.\n(2) Affected residents are located between 17m and 45m from the roadway to which\na target L of 55dBA applies, according to DECCW’s Road Noise Policy.\n(3) An increase of around 2dB would be experienced by the residents, which\ncomplies with the upper limit of 2dB, according to Section A.4 ‘Construction\nrelated traffic noise objectives’ of the Construction Noise and Vibration\nGuidelines, issued by Transport for NSW, September 2023 (Construction Noise\nObjectives).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":48},{"label":"260","code":null,"number":"260","title":"The Acoustic Report recommends two measures be adopted to manage the acoustic","text":"impact:\n(1) Deliveries should be made by a truck, with dog to lower the number of trips and,\nas a consequence, the L noise levels experienced by residents.\n(2) Truck braking using engine-compression be avoided within 100m of any\nresidence on Old Maitland Road.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":48},{"label":"261","code":null,"number":"261","title":"","text":"Between the site and the Tuggerah Interchange, the experts agree there are five, not\nfour, dwellings that front Old Maitland Road, according to aerial images prepared by\nStevens (Exhibit H):\n(1) No 449 Old Maitland Road is opposite the site, on the eastern side of the road,\nwith a 55m setback.\n(2) No 427 Old Maitland Road is a fibre cement dwelling that is likewise on the\nAeq\nAeq\nAeq-1hr\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 48/80\neastern side of the roadway, with a setback of 17m.\n(3) Located close to No 427 Old Maitland Road is No 10 Colliers Lane, a fibre\ncement weatherboard dwelling with a setback of 61m.\n(4) No 343 Old Maitland Road is a fibre cement dwelling setback 23m from the\nroadway.\n(5) No 333 Old Maitland Road is a dwelling with a setback of 30m from the\nroadway.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":48},{"label":"262","code":null,"number":"262","title":"","text":"Mr Brindle’s oral evidence is that a key difference between the Acoustic Report and the\nTraffic Report is the number of truck trips in the Traffic Report should be regarded\n‘worst case scenario’ whereas the number of truck trips resulting from the measures\nproposed by Mr Leebruggen’s at [260] would reduce the number of truck movements\nbecause every truck would also carry a trailer, known commonly as a ‘dog’.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":49},{"label":"263","code":null,"number":"263","title":"","text":"Furthermore, while the Traffic Report assumes a constant flow of trucks carrying fill to\nthe site, in reality this would be determined by variables such as the availability of\nappropriate fill, the marketing, sales and construction of the stages proposed. While this\nwould not alter the amount of fill, it would likely reduce the frequency of truck\nmovements over time.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":49},{"label":"264","code":null,"number":"264","title":"The Council essentially submits that the proposal adopts two conflicting standards for","text":"development fronting Old Maitland Road. Where development is proposed on the\nsubject site, future dwellings fronting Old Maitland Road are identified with solid\nconstruction designed to withstand acoustic impact from traffic travelling on that\nroadway, whereas the acoustic assessment adopted for the five dwellings at [261] fails\nto consider the construction and acoustic performance of existing dwellings.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":49},{"label":"265","code":null,"number":"265","title":"","text":"Mr Brindle is of the view that as the Acoustic Report identifies the relevant noise criteria\nare achieved, an assessment of internal acoustic performance is not required.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":49},{"label":"266","code":null,"number":"266","title":"","text":"I accept the assessment of acoustic impact at [259(3)] complies with the Construction\nNoise Objectives, albeit at the upper end of the range, and that the quantum of truck\nmovements forecast by the figures at either [255] or [260] are likely to be discontinuous\nand separated by time as the commencement of stages is so often likewise\ndiscontinuous.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 49/80\nWhether avoidance and minimisation of impact on biodiversity values is evident","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":49},{"label":"267","code":null,"number":"267","title":"The Council contends that the proposed development does not adequately","text":"demonstrate measures that avoid or minimise impact on the biodiversity values of the\nsite itself, or in those areas impacted off-site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"268","code":null,"number":"268","title":"","text":"An aspect of the contention is the impact of slashing on the biodiversity values of the\nsite, dealt with at [67]-[68].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"269","code":null,"number":"269","title":"","text":"Another is the failure by the proposal to demonstrate avoidance of widespread clearing,\nor the inverse, which is failure to retain trees.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"270","code":null,"number":"270","title":"The Council also contends that the Trigger Action Response Plan (TARP), contained","text":"within the BMP fails to set out the nature of intervention to assure that a functional\necosystem will be returned on the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"271","code":null,"number":"271","title":"","text":"The Council further contends that the BDARs prepared by Ms Black do not adequately\naddress the requirement to ‘avoid’ and ‘minimise’ the impact on biodiversity values of\nthe site in the manner set out in Section 7.1.1 of the BAM (Exhibit B, tab 8).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"272","code":null,"number":"272","title":"","text":"An assessment of direct and relevant indirect impacts is contained in Section 2.2 of the\nBDAR, set out in Tables 21 to 24, as required by Section 8 of the BAM.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"273","code":null,"number":"273","title":"","text":"In each of the Tables, the particular aspect or impact is described against the project\nphase, being in terms of pre or post construction, or later once the proposed\ndevelopment is in operation. Mitigation strategies, and the likely timing of those\nstrategies, are also set out, along with the responsibility for those strategies.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"274","code":null,"number":"274","title":"","text":"Section 7.1.1 of the BAM states:\n“7.1.1 Locate the proposal to avoid or minimise direct and indirect impacts onnative vegetation, threatened species, threatened ecological communities andtheir habitat\n1. Knowledge of biodiversity values should inform decisions about the location of theproposal. The initial assessment of biodiversity values from Stage 1 may be used toinform the early planning of the route or location of a proposal.\n2. Selecting a final proposal location may be an iterative process. Decisions may needto be revisited after all field surveys have been completed.\n3. Impacts from clearing native vegetation and threatened species habitat can beavoided or minimised by locating the proposal in areas:\na. lacking biodiversity values\nb. where the native vegetation or threatened species habitat is in the poorestcondition (i.e. areas that have a low vegetation integrity score)\nc. that avoid habitat for species with a high biodiversity risk weighting or landmapped on the important habitat map, or native vegetation that is a TEC or ahighly cleared PCT.\nd. outside of the buffer area around breeding habitat features such as nest treesor caves.\n4. When selecting a proposal’s location, all of the following should be analysed.Justification for the decisions in determining the final location must be based onconsideration of:\na. alternative modes or technologies that would avoid or minimise impacts onbiodiversity values\nb. alternative routes that would avoid or minimise impacts on biodiversity values\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 50/80\nc. alternative locations that would avoid or minimise impacts on biodiversityvalues\nd. alternative sites within a property on which the proposal is located that wouldavoid or minimise impacts on biodiversity values.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":50},{"label":"275","code":null,"number":"275","title":"","text":"At paragraph 7.1.1(6), there is also a requirement that “the assessor must document\nand justify any actions taken to avoid or minimise impacts through careful location of\nthe proposal.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"276","code":null,"number":"276","title":"","text":"Dr McLean believes that the extent of clearing proposed has the potential to result in a\nmajority of native vegetation being cleared in the subdivision area zoned C3. This is\ninconsistent with the zone objectives as there is no certainty that the proposed\nrestoration activities will create special ecological values.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"277","code":null,"number":"277","title":"","text":"I understand Dr McLean’s reference to be a reference to the first objective of the zone\nbeing “To protect, manage and restore areas of high ecological…values.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"278","code":null,"number":"278","title":"","text":"Dr McLean believes the impact of such clearing will be to remove or modify the\nbreeding habitat of the Green-Thighed Frog, and no measures to support the re-\nintroduction of the species in such land is evident in the BMP. The consequence could\nbe the extinction of the species, notwithstanding habitat for the Green-thighed frog in\nland zoned C2.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"279","code":null,"number":"279","title":"","text":"Ms Black’s evidence is that the habitat cited by Dr McLean is degraded and currently\nsubject to grazing pressure. A key to restoring fish habitat and a functioning aquatic\nsystem is to de-stock the site of grazing animals, and the restoration and reinstatement\nof a stable bed and banks, and those features such a pools, riffles and meanders\n(Exhibit 6, par 170) as is now proposed within the BMP lands. Such a habitat will\nimprove the breeding habitat for the Green-thighed frog, over that of the artificial\ndrainage channel evident today.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"280","code":null,"number":"280","title":"","text":"In respect of the Powerful Owl and Sooty Owl, Dr McLean asserts that insufficient effort\nhas been made to identify trees suited to the breeding of those species, with no nest\nidentified that would require a buffer of 300m to be observed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"281","code":null,"number":"281","title":"","text":"As Ms Black’s evidence is that the buffer’s proposed, such as the 10m buffer is not\nprovided to demonstrate avoidance, but of the minimisation of impacts as it is proposed\nto be managed so as to achieve targets set out in Tables 1, 2 and 3 of the BMP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"282","code":null,"number":"282","title":"","text":"In my view, and in ways summarised as follows, the BDAR does adequately make out\nsteps taken by Stevens to avoid and minimise the impact of the development on the\nbiodiversity values of the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"283","code":null,"number":"283","title":"","text":"The Subdivision BDAR sets out guiding principles that I understand to represent steps\ntaken to avoid or minimise adverse impacts of the proposed development on the land.\nThese principles include, non-exhaustively, the removal of grazing stock; protection,\nregeneration and reinstatement of fauna habitat; the trial treatment for Myrtle Rust in\nthe Scrub Turpentine community that has the potential to remove the species from the\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 51/80\ncritical endangered species list; improvement of water quality by proposed stormwater\nsystems, and community education programs in the Biodiversity Stewardship site.\n(Exhibit M, Tab 1, folio 147).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":51},{"label":"284","code":null,"number":"284","title":"The Subdivision BDAR states that the development generally minimises the impact on","text":"the site by virtue of the land reserved in the Biodiversity Stewardship site, and in the\nland managed by the BMP. However, in more relevant terms, the Subdivision BDAR\nrelies on the following measures designed to avoid and minimise the adverse impacts\non the biodiversity values of the site (pp 151-158):\n(1) Identification of BMP lands on the site, to be managed in accordance with the\nBMP.\n(2) Implementation of a fungicide treatment and monitoring program of the Scrub\nTurpentine and translocation of the Scrub Turpentine and Magenta Lilly Pilly into\nBMP lands which minimises the impact of development on these species.\n(3) Preparation of a Tree protection plan within the C3 zoned land and in the vicinity\nof Deep Creek.\n(4) Adoption of the Biodiversity Stewardship Site Assessment Report (BSSAR),\napproved by the Credit Supply Taskforce, to manage 139.85 hectares of intact\nremnant vegetation.\n(5) Completion of Bushfire Threat and Riparian Assessments, and Arboricultural\nimpact assessments for servicing of the site in the services corridor and the\nintersection upgrade.\n(6) Independent peer review of the proposal by Eastcoast Flora Survey (Exhibit M,\nAppendix N) that resulted in initiatives that further avoid or minimise the impact\nof development in respect of Melaleuca biconvexa, Scrub Turpentine and\nMagenta Lilly Pilly.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":52},{"label":"285","code":null,"number":"285","title":"","text":"According to section 2.1.5 of the Subdivision BDAR, steps taken by Stevens to avoid\nand minimise the impact on Scrub Turpentine include the removal of lots from the\nsubdivision plan that contributes to the retention of all but 7 of the individuals, and the\nidentification of a high density cluster in the south west corner of the site to be\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 52/80\nmanaged under the BMP, that is further protected by the proposed 10m buffer to the C2\nland. These steps are contrasted with the ‘do nothing’ approach that is highly likely to\nresult in the loss of the species due to Myrtle Rust.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":52},{"label":"286","code":null,"number":"286","title":"","text":"Further steps are also set out in a letter prepared by AEP dated 5 July 2024 (AEP\nletter) (Exhibit A, Tab U). In essence, the AEP letter argues that the reduction in the\nnumber of lots proposed within the south-west corner of the site reduces the impact on\nthe Scrub Turpentine, supported by a schedule of measures taken to avoid and\nminimise the impacts on the species.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"287","code":null,"number":"287","title":"","text":"Given the high density of Scrub Turpentine in the south west corner, the AEP letter\nsummarises an exercise of option analysis that resulted in an area of vegetation\nproposed to be retained, adjoining the 10m tree protection buffer.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"288","code":null,"number":"288","title":"Three hundred and forty Scrub Turpentine individuals are identified within the","text":"Biodiversity Stewardship site, and the development site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"289","code":null,"number":"289","title":"","text":"The AEP Letter states that, as a result of amendments to the development in July 2024,\n28 of the 40 individuals identified within the site boundary will be retained and managed\nwithin the BMP land.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"290","code":null,"number":"290","title":"","text":"An additional 3 individuals are also now retained within the site boundary as a result of\nchanging the crossing of Deep Creek by means of two bridges, in place of the culverts\noriginally proposed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"291","code":null,"number":"291","title":"","text":"The AEP Letter distils the effect of steps taken to avoid or minimise results in a change\nin the number of individuals impacted from 46 as originally proposed, to 12 being either\nimpacted or translocated in the further amended DA.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"292","code":null,"number":"292","title":"","text":"These measures are said to result in 96.5% of the species in the study area being\nretained, and for all of the retained individuals to be subject to management, monitoring\nand treatment in the form of fungicide to counter Myrtle Rust.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"293","code":null,"number":"293","title":"","text":"A letter prepared by AEP dated 7 March 2024 (Exhibit A, Tab W) provides a summary of\nthe arborists assessment of the interface between the land zoned R5 and C2. The\nassessment describes the nature of the assessment made, including the application of\ntree protection zones intended to limit incursion into TPZs of more than 10%, and so\nanticipate no removal of trees as a consequence of the interface. A further setback of\n500mm is proposed where earthworks are proposed.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"294","code":null,"number":"294","title":"","text":"An updated letter dated 13 September 2024 (Exhibit M, Tab 6) notes the additional\nwork by a surveyor to locate all trees in the vicinity of the 10m buffer. Twenty three\ntrees are identified for removal, and biodiversity credits are now applied.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"295","code":null,"number":"295","title":"As to whether the TARP adequately provides for intervention in circumstances where","text":"regeneration of ecosystems on the site, as contended by the Council, (Exhibit M, Tab 5,\npp 51-59):\n(1) The TARP enumerates nine issues or threats that have the potential for\nsignificant impact on the ecosystem proposed to be regenerated, including fire,\nflood, drought, weed infestation, pathogens, pest species, reduction in native\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 53/80\nfauna, vehicle strike and vandalism or theft.\n(2) Table 20 sets out four general targets that, if not achieved, would trigger actions\nand responses led by a bush regeneration contractor or project ecologist of a\nkind included in the table.\n(3) Table 21 identifies triggers, actions and responses should targets set out for\nthose nine issues or threats at [295(1)] fail to be achieved.\n(4) I note that where the cause of an aspect or element cannot be identified, or is\ndifficult to determine, Table 20 also provides for responses that are to be\nundertaken.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":53},{"label":"296","code":null,"number":"296","title":"","text":"The Council has not identified any particular issues or threats that are lacking in the\nTARP. Where particular issues of concern to Dr McLean are addressed in the TARP,\nsuch as the potential for bare soil to result along the naturalised drainage channel, or\nlack of a watering regime to those trees retained in the C3 zone during construction\nworks (see [237]), Table 20 would appear to provide identified actions and responses\nsuch as the addition of mulch or leaf litter up to year three of the BMP. Table 21 also\nprovides various actions and responses that include monitoring and assessment of\nconditions and provide for the preparation in certain circumstances of new planting\nschedules, mulching and watering and the like.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":54},{"label":"297","code":null,"number":"297","title":"","text":"It also appears relevant to Dr McLean’s concern that bare soil may result along the\nnaturalised drainage channel that the flooding, engineering and ecology experts,\nincluding Dr McLean, agree that the velocities predicted in the watercourses on the site\nare acceptable when the potential impact downstream are factored (Exhibit 6, par 52).\nA contributing factor to this agreement is the position jointly advanced by Mr Thomas\nand Mr Brien that the velocities are reduced from those currently on the site, and that\nthe velocities downstream do not reach the threshold to erode grass.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":54},{"label":"298","code":null,"number":"298","title":"Design measures taken to avoid and minimise impacts on Bioconvex Paperbark and","text":"Magenta Lilly Pilly are also set out in Section 2.1.3 that are in similar terms to those\nmeasures described in respect of Scrub Turpentine. This includes the 10m buffer\naround the perimeter of the site adjoining the C2 land, the area of land contained within\nthe BMP lands that is zoned C3 and R5, and by adopting tree protection measures,\nweed management and supervision by a qualified arborist.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":54},{"label":"299","code":null,"number":"299","title":"","text":"Just as for scrub Turpentine, Magenta Lilly Pilly is also likely to benefit from the\ncessation of grazing on the site, and so impacts on the individuals be minimised by this\nsource of threat that is described in the National Recovery Plan for Magenta Lilly Pilly\nJune 2012 (National Recovery Plan) (Exhibit Q) as a contributing factor in the decline of\nsome of the largest subpopulations along the Ourimbah River and in the Martinsville\narea (p 14).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":54},{"label":"300","code":null,"number":"300","title":"","text":"For completeness, I note here that a concern of Dr McLean’s which goes unresolved in\nmy mind is his insistence that the population size found in the Final Determination\nappears at odds with that contained in the National Recovery Plan and that both are, in\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 54/80\nhis view, incorrect. Instead, Dr McLean cites his own work to arrive at a prediction he\nconsiders more reliable, and which suggests an Area of Occurrence at odds with the\nFinal Determination and the National Recovery Plan. It is on this basis that Dr McLean\nurges the Court to proceed on the basis of the precautionary principle - presumably\nsetting aside the advice of the Final Determination and guidance found in the National\nRecovery Plan. I decline to do so, but rely upon data contained in the Final\nDetermination and the National Recovery Plan.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":54},{"label":"301","code":null,"number":"301","title":"","text":"In respect of the fauna, the Subdivision BDAR asserts the impact of the development is\nminimised, primarily, by the proposed increase in quality and diversity of habitat in the\nland zoned C2 and C3, and to areas within the R5 zoned land that are to be managed\nunder the BMP. This is achieved by limiting lighting and vehicle speeds within the\ndevelopment, and by improving the water quality in Deep Creek and the proposed\nreinstated tributary, with watering points for native species. The installation of habitat in\nthe form of hollows and nesting boxes will support native wildlife, and waterway\nrestoration will provide open waterbodies suited to habitat for Myotis bats.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":55},{"label":"302","code":null,"number":"302","title":"","text":"The AIA prepared by AEP for the proposed Subdivision and dated September 2024\nstates that the Stage 5 Bridge, in the vicinity of the Magenta Lilly Pilly, is likely to require\nthe removal of 76 trees, comprising 23 with a high retention value, 50 with a moderate\nretention value and 3 assessed as low or very low retention value.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":55},{"label":"303","code":null,"number":"303","title":"","text":"A further 43 trees are either outside the likely development footprint, or are within close\nproximity but may be retained subject to appropriate tree protection measures.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":55},{"label":"304","code":null,"number":"304","title":"","text":"Dr Mclean’s written evidence is that Appendix A of the AIA shows no incursion in to the\nTPZ or SRZ of trees in the vicinity of the Stage 5 bridges. However, while noting he has\nno qualifications in the field of arboriculture, Dr Mclean speculates that tree decline,\ncollapse or death is possible, which would have the effect of reducing the ecological\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 55/80\nfunctionalism of the wildlife corridor proposed in this area of the site. Should this occur,\nthe impacts of development on the site would not be minimised. (Exhibit 12, par 452-\n453)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":55},{"label":"305","code":null,"number":"305","title":"","text":"Mr Muir’s evidence is that trees within the vicinity of the vehicles crossing of Deep\nCreek can be protected according to a methodology he sets out in the joint report\n(Exhibit 12, para 455), and by proposed conditions of consent. In his evidence, Mr Muir\nrelies on a description of the scour protection proposed in this area of Deep Creek\nprovided by Northrop Engineers.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":56},{"label":"306","code":null,"number":"306","title":"","text":"I note here that the flooding, engineering and ecology experts record their agreement\nthat potential for stream erosion and measures to provide scour protection have been\nconsidered in the design, and that the velocity of stream flow does not have an adverse\nimpact on bank erosion or on velocities downstream, and so resolves certain\ncontentions originally pressed by the Council. (Exhibit 6, par 50)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":56},{"label":"307","code":null,"number":"307","title":"","text":"The AIA sets out an assessment of trees in the vicinity of the proposed scour protection\nworks, and outlines measures for those trees that are proposed for retention. (Exhibit\nM, Vol 3, Section 6.3)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":56},{"label":"308","code":null,"number":"308","title":"","text":"In respect of the assessment of trees in both the Subdivision and Services Corridor, I\nnote the summary at Table 1 of the letter prepared by AEP dated 13 September 2024\n(Exhibit M, Tab 8) advises the total change in tree impact arising from the assessment\ncompleted by ADWJ surveyors is as follows:\n(1) In respect of development proposed in the Services corridor: 1 additional tree is\nto be retained\n(2) In respect of the development proposed associated with the subdivision: 26\nadditional trees are to be retained.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":56},{"label":"309","code":null,"number":"309","title":"","text":"Finally, I am satisfied that Stevens is able to demonstrate that the proposed\ndevelopment has achieved a degree of avoidance and minimisation of impacts by\nreference to those three plans that respectively depict the area of land subject to\nrezoning cited at [29], compared with that area of land after the Stewardship Site is\nexcised, and that which is the area of land now proposed for development on R5 zoned\nland, re-produced below (Exhibit A, Tab 28).\nFigure 6: First proposed area of the site subject to R5 rezoning.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 56/80\nFigure 7: Second proposed area of the site subject to R5 rezoning.\nFigure 8: Final proposed area of the site subject to R5 rezoning.\nWhether the removal of trees will have Serious and Irreversible Impacts on threatened\nspecies and communities","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":56},{"label":"310","code":null,"number":"310","title":"","text":"Given the extent of native vegetation proposed to be cleared, and the extent of\nearthworks proposed on the site, it is Council’s submission that serious and irreversible\nimpacts will result on the EEC’s present on the site, such that consent must be refused\npursuant to s 7.16(2) of the BC Act.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":57},{"label":"311","code":null,"number":"311","title":"","text":"I note here that while the BDARs prepared by AEP assess three species for the risk of\nserious and irreversible impacts (SAII), only the Scrub Turpentine in identified in the\nBCAR as a SAII entity.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":57},{"label":"312","code":null,"number":"312","title":"","text":"Section 7.16 of the BC Act requires the Court to refuse to grant consent to the\ndevelopment application if it is of the opinion that the development is likely to have\n‘serious and irreversible impacts on biodiversity values’. Section 7.16 relevantly\nprovides:\n7.16 Proposed development or activity that has serious and irreversible impactson biodiversity values\n(1) In this section, serious and irreversible impacts on biodiversity values of proposeddevelopment or activity means serious and irreversible impacts on biodiversity valuesas determined under section 6.5 that would remain after the measures proposed to betaken to avoid or minimise the impact on biodiversity values of the proposeddevelopment or activity.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 57/80\n(2) The consent authority must refuse to grant consent under Part 4 of theEnvironmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979, in the case of an application fordevelopment consent to which this Division applies (other than for State significantdevelopment), if it is of the opinion that the proposed development is likely to haveserious and irreversible impacts on biodiversity values.\n…\n(4) If the determining authority is of the opinion that the proposed activity to which thisDivision applies is likely to have serious and irreversible impacts on biodiversity values,the determining authority-\n(a) is required to take those impacts into consideration, and\n(b) is required to determine whether there are any additional and appropriatemeasures that will minimise those impacts if the activity is to be carried out orapproved.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":57},{"label":"313","code":null,"number":"313","title":"","text":"The Council contends that, absent an assessment of the risk of Serious and Irreversible\nImpacts on these EEC’s, the Court cannot be satisfied that a Serious and Irreversible\nImpact will not result.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":58},{"label":"314","code":null,"number":"314","title":"","text":"An assessment of the risk of Serious and Irreversible Impacts arising from the\nproposed development has been undertaken in the BDARs, which are documents to\nwhich the Court must direct its attention when considering the likely impact of the\nproposed development on biodiversity values (s 7.13(2) of the BC Act).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":58},{"label":"315","code":null,"number":"315","title":"","text":"For reasons that I will shortly set out, I am satisfied that the BDARs prepared in support\nof the development application, as amended, have been prepared in accordance with s\n6.12 of the BC Act, are consistent with the guidance provided in the BAM and the\ndevelopment will not result in a serious and irreversible impact on the relevant species.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":58},{"label":"316","code":null,"number":"316","title":"Section 7.16(1) provides guidance as to how the determination of Serious and","text":"Irreversible Impacts on biodiversity values is to be made by reference to s 6.5 of the BC\nAct which is in the following terms:\n6.5 Serious and irreversible impacts on biodiversity values\n(1) The determination of serious and irreversible impacts on biodiversity values for thepurposes of the biodiversity offsets scheme is to be made in accordance with principlesprescribed by the regulations.\n(2) The Environment Agency Head may provide guidance on the determination of anysuch serious and irreversible impacts, and for that purpose may publish, from time totime, criteria to assist in the application of those principles and lists of potential seriousand irreversible impacts.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":58},{"label":"317","code":null,"number":"317","title":"","text":"The regulations referred to in s 6.5(1) of the BC Act are found at cl 6.7 of the BC\nRegulation as follows:\n6.7 Principles applicable to determination of “serious and irreversible impacts onbiodiversity values” (section 6.5(1))\n(1) This clause applies for the purposes of determining whether an impact on diversityvalues is a serious and irreversible impact for the purposes of the biodiversity offsetsscheme.\n(2) An impact is to be regarded as serious and irreversible if it is likely to contributesignificantly to the risk of a threatened species or ecological community becomingextinct because-\n(a) it will cause a further decline of the species or ecological community that iscurrently observed, estimated, inferred or reasonably suspected to be in a rapidrate of decline, or\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 58/80\n(b) it will further reduce the population size of the species or ecologicalcommunity that is currently observed, estimated, inferred or reasonablysuspected to have a very small population size, or\n(c) it is an impact on the habitat of the species or ecological community that iscurrently observed, estimated, inferred or reasonably suspected to have a verylimited geographic distribution, or\n(d) the impacted species or ecological community is unlikely to respond tomeasures to improve its habitat and vegetation integrity and therefore itsmembers are not replaceable.\n(3) For the purpose of this clause, a decline of a species or ecological community is acontinuing or projected decline in-\n(a) an index of abundance appropriate to the taxon, or\n(b) the geographic distribution and habitat quality of the species or ecologicalcommunity.\n…","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":58},{"label":"318","code":null,"number":"318","title":"A detailed assessment of the likelihood of serious and irreversible impacts on","text":"biodiversity values of the Scrub Turpentine against the principles found at s 6.7 of the\nBC Regulation is at Table 32 of the BDAR.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":59},{"label":"319","code":null,"number":"319","title":"","text":"I also note the provisions at Section 9.1.2 of the BAM are used in Table 32 in providing\na framework for the assessment.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":59},{"label":"320","code":null,"number":"320","title":"","text":"In respect of the Principle at s 6.7(2)(a):\n(1) the species has recently been listed as critically endangered as a population\nreduction of more than 80% is projected over three generations or 10 years due\nto Myrtle Rust, first detected in 2010.\n(2) Myrtle Rust is responsible for the low rate of maturing from seedlings and young\nsaplings into mature trees.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":59},{"label":"321","code":null,"number":"321","title":"","text":"In respect of Principle s 6.7(2)(b):\n(1) The Extent of Occurrence (EOO) was estimated to be 147,340km (Exhibit M,\nFigure 48), and the Area of Occupancy (AOO) estimated to be 3,360km (Exhibit\nM, Figure 49) in 2018.\n(2) No specific population size was recorded prior to the onset of Myrtle Rust and\nso the severity of decline is unknown. However, studies have found that the\nMyrtle Rust affects all age classes of the species and there is ample evidence of\na relatively rapid decline in numbers since 2010.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":59},{"label":"322","code":null,"number":"322","title":"","text":"In respect of Principle s 6.7(2)(c):\n(1) Scrub Turpentine has an extensive natural distribution, and a total of 2,740\nrecords associated with the species. The distribution appears reasonably\ncontiguous with no significant disjunctions.\n(2) Neither the EOO or the AOO of the Scrub Turpentine meet the threshold\nadopted by the International Union for Conservation (IUCN) for geographically\nrestricted distribution, nor does the species exhibit two of the three\n2\n2\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 59/80\ncharacteristics including being severely fragmented or only known from one\nlocation or experiencing continuing decline, and does not inhabit less than or\nequal to three locations in NSW.\n(3) Given the ease with which Myrtle Rust can be dispersed by wind, the population\nappears affected across all geographic ranges and age classes.\n(4) However, the Carnegie Study has shown that trees treated with a fungicide\nrecovered from Myrtle Rust over those that were untreated.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":59},{"label":"323","code":null,"number":"323","title":"","text":"In respect of Principle s 6.7(2)(d):\n(1) The Carnegie Study found that a tree experiencing repeated, severe infection\nwith Myrtle Rust can eventually lead to death of the individual. However,\napplication of fungicide can stem infection and reduce mortality. It is proposed to\nadopt a regime of applying the fungicide to the Scrub Turpentine located in the\nBiodiversity Stewardship Site.\n(2) Regular application of the fungicide is likely to ensure ongoing survival of the\nindividuals, including the 12 individuals proposed to be translocated from the\ndevelopment site to the Biodiversity Stewardship Site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":60},{"label":"324","code":null,"number":"324","title":"","text":"The detailed assessment in Table 32 also considers those matters that are not explicitly\nrequired by the principles at s 6.7 of the BC Regulation, but are set out in the guidance\nin Section 9.1.2 of the BAM.\n(1) The BDAR acknowledges that the data derived from the Threatened Biodiversity\nData Collection found in the BioNet database for Scrub Turpentine is limited,\nconsistent with the advice at Item 3 for the assessor to record such a finding.\n(2) Likewise, the BDAR includes data and information on the proposed and\nprojected impact on Scrub Turpentine in terms similar to those set out in the\nAEP Letter at [286]-[291], consistent with the advice at Item 4a. The resultant\nimpact is said to be negligible, as the decline in populations will be offset by the\ntranslocation and the management of Myrtle Rust by fungicide over a 20 year\nperiod.\n(3) In respect of the impact on the geographic range of Scrub Turpentine, being the\nsubject of item 4a, the BDAR proceeds on the premise that the species was\ncommon prior to the outbreak of Myrtle Rust. The number of individuals is said\nto be not less than 10,000 and for reasons stated earlier, the impact is likely to\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 60/80\nbe negligible. The statement on p 269 of Table 32 acknowledges that whole-\nplant translocation for Scrub Turpentine is unproven, however similar species\nhave been translocated.\n(4) Finally, the information contained in Table 32 addresses item 5 of the Section by\nnoting a thorough search resulted in no new information that identified SAII risks\nfor Scrub Turpentine, or that indicates the subject site would put Scrub\nTurpentine at risk of an SAII.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":60},{"label":"325","code":null,"number":"325","title":"","text":"Dr Mclean believes, absent current published data on the subject, it is likely that the\nRFEF of which Scrub Turpentine is a part is in further decline due to additional clearing\nsince its listing in 2004 and that his own observations suggest there is insufficient\ninformation to rule out those parts of the site that are severely degraded from being part\nof the RFEF or SSF communities.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":61},{"label":"326","code":null,"number":"326","title":"","text":"For reasons set out at par 291 of the joint report, Dr McLean considers it reasonable to\nconclude the total loss of Scrub Turpentine since 1788 has been rapid and that a loss\nof 70% of vegetation within the RFEF community is likely. Dr Mclean cites a range of\n10.09-19.19 hectares of RFEF proposed to be cleared. The area varies because of the\ndifference between the experts as to whether areas of the site identified as RFEF are\nfactored in or out, due to the condition mapped in the BDAR, and the resulting VIS.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":61},{"label":"327","code":null,"number":"327","title":"","text":"As stated at [67]-[68], I have found the VIS to arise from land management practices\nassociated with the long use of the site for farming. The result is a degraded PCT,\nparticularly in those areas of open grassland. It follows that I accept Ms Black’s lower\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 61/80\nestimate of 10.09 hectares that are proposed to be cleared, comprising 4.79 hectares\nof PCT mapped in good and moderate condition, 3.32 hectares mapped in poor\ncondition, and 1.98 hectares mapped as highly degraded.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":61},{"label":"328","code":null,"number":"328","title":"","text":"I accept the proposition put by Stevens that a decline in Scrub Turpentine is not likely to\nbe an outcome of the proposed development, given the small number of individuals\nproposed to be affected by the proposed development. Instead, the decline is of a\ndifferent origin, namely Myrtle Rust.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"329","code":null,"number":"329","title":"","text":"It is relevant to note that the Scientific Committee is of the view that the RFEF\ncommunity is likely to become extinct in nature in NSW unless circumstances and\nfactors threatening its survival cease (Exhibit 14).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"330","code":null,"number":"330","title":"","text":"There is evidence to support the effectiveness of fungicide treatment on the effect of\nMrytle Rust on Scrub Turpentine, and a regime of treatment is proposed. As such, there\nis potential for Myrtle Rust, as a factor threatening Scrub Turpentine, to reduce, albeit\nnot cease, on this site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"331","code":null,"number":"331","title":"","text":"I accept the assessment summarised at [318]-[324] against the principles at s 6.7 of the\nBC Regulation. In particular I accept that Scrub Turpentine does not have a\ngeographically restricted distribution according to the threshold adopted by the\nInternational Union for Conservation (IUCN), nor is the species severely fragmented or\nonly known from one location, but is found at more than three locations in NSW.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"332","code":null,"number":"332","title":"","text":"Given the small number of individuals that are affected by the proposal, and the\nfungicide treatment proposed by Stevens over a 20 year period, I find the proposed\ndevelopment will not result in a serious and irreversible impact on the Scrub Turpentine\nspecies, but may yield positive results for understanding a method of maintenance and\nmanagement of the Myrtle Rust that certainly threatens the species.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"333","code":null,"number":"333","title":"","text":"I also record here that due to the small number of Magenta Lilly Pilly affected by the\nproposal, and the extent of occurrence and geographic distribution are properly\nunderstood, I can only conclude the proposed development will not have a SAII on that\nspecies.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"334","code":null,"number":"334","title":"","text":"Likewise, while not identified in the BCAR as a SAII entity, an assessment of such risk\nin respect of Biconvex Paperbark is also included in the BDARs. The extent of\nBiconvex Paperbark on the site is stated at [108], and the AOO of illustrated at Figure\n45 of the BDAR. Stevens submits that the extent of impact on the species when\nunderstood as a proportion of the area of occurrence, may be expressed at 0.0018%.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"335","code":null,"number":"335","title":"","text":"For completeness, I note an assessment of the risk of SAII for Magenta Lilly Pilly is at\nAttachment C of the joint ecology report (Exhibit 12), stating that no individuals are\nproposed to be removed from the Services Corridor.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"336","code":null,"number":"336","title":"","text":"In my view, the BDARs prepared in support of the proposal sufficiently enable the Court\nto assess the likely impact of the development and the number and class of biodiversity\ncredits to be retired to offset the impacts that arise from the development.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"337","code":null,"number":"337","title":"Stevens has demonstrated that the impacts arising from the proposed development","text":"02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 62/80\nhave been, in part, avoided or minimised, and that no serious and irreversible impacts\nare expected on those species assessed in accordance with s 6.5 of the BC Act, and\nthe principles at s 6.7 of the BC Regulation, following the avoidance and minimisation\nachieved.\nThe land is bushfire prone land","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":62},{"label":"338","code":null,"number":"338","title":"The Council contends the development should be refused because the proposed road","text":"providing access to both Stage 5 and Old Maitland Road is unacceptable due to the\ninability to safely evacuate in the instance of a bush fire to the north or south.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":63},{"label":"339","code":null,"number":"339","title":"","text":"The proposed access road is shown traversing private roads, and a watercourse that\ncontains native vegetation. Furthermore, a secondary access is intended to provide an\nalternative option to primary access. However, the two access roads are located within\n200m of each other and are in the same geographical area, meaning that both could be\naffected by bushfire, and so defeating the purpose of the access.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":63},{"label":"340","code":null,"number":"340","title":"","text":"The Court was assisted in its consideration of bushfire threat and mitigation by the\nevidence of the following bushfire experts:\nMr Stuart McMonnies (for the Applicant)\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 63/80\nDr Chris McLean and Brendan Dee (for the Council)","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":63},{"label":"341","code":null,"number":"341","title":"","text":"The experts conferred in the preparation of a joint expert report (Exhibit 4), in which\nthey record agreement as follows:\n(1) Stage 5 has the greatest level of exposure to bushfire prone land. This is due to\nthe Ourimbah State Forest and Jilliby State Conservation Area to the west (circa\n20,000 ha). The prevailing weather conditions associated with the bushfire\nseason on the Central Coast are high daytime temperatures and low humidity\naccompanied by north-westerly winds.\n(2) Vehicle access in a south-easterly direction from Stage 5 is logical from a\nbushfire planning perspective due to:\n(a) Prevailing weather conditions;\n(b) Relatively short travel distance before further protection is provided by\nthe balance of the subdivision;\n(c) Other directions would result large travel distances through Forest\nvegetation.\n(3) The second access available from Stage 5 provides additional traffic capacity\nprior to bushfire impact when public messaging would be to ‘leave early’. The\nroads also provide additional operational benefits for attending fire services and\nan alternate access route if one of the roads is compromised (e.g. vehicle\naccident, fallen tree, etc).\n(4) At the time of impact of a large-scale bushfire to which Planning for Bush Fire\nProtection 2019 (PBP) is intended to mitigate multiple roads throughout the\ndevelopment and broader landscape may be the subject of direct impact, and\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 64/80\nnot suitable for use at that time. It is acknowledged that at this time members of\nthe public will be actively directed by emergency services not to evacuate due to\nthe inherent danger.\n(5) The application of PBP provides minimum building setbacks to the identified\nhazards and corresponding construction measures for future dwelling\napplications. These measures afford buildings and their occupants protection\nfrom the exposure to a bushfire within the design limitations, in the event early\nevacuation could not be achieved or property owners elect to ‘stay and defend’.\n(6) For Stage 5, the experts agree that to provide further confidence that the future\ndwellings will afford occupants protection from exposure to bushfire that Bushfire\nAttack Levels (BAL) 19 setbacks and BAL 29 construction standards are\nspecified on title and in without prejudice Conditions of Consent. This is beyond\nPBP requirements at section 5.1.1 of PBP 2019.\n(7) To provide an ability for occupants to be more self-sufficient the experts agree\nthat static water supplies (min 5000L per lot) and petrol/ diesel pump with hoses\nare included on every residential lot of Stage 5.\n(8) Annual training is available for all residents of the association on building\npreparedness, APZ management and use of firefighting equipment. Attendance\nat training is not compulsory but encouraged, with sufficient notice provided to\nmembers when training occurs. This needs to be reflected in the Community\nManagement Statement or similar.\n(9) The experts agree with the Conditions provided by the NSW RFS that specify\nemergency access is for use during emergency events only. The experts agree\nthat the emergency access is not to be gated to ensure reliable and efficient\naccess is provided in the event of an emergency response. “Emergency Access\nOnly” signage shall be provided at the emergency access at all times.\n(10) The Community Management Statement is to state that the emergency access\nis to be used for emergency use only. Other use would be considered\ninconsistent with the by laws of the association and could be enforceable\nwithout a valid reason.\n(11) A condition of consent should require every occupied residential lot to prepare a\nBushfire Survival Plan. The Bushfire Survival Plan is to be reviewed after a\nchange of ownership/tenancy, change of circumstances, after a bushfire event\nor on a voluntary basis. Encouragement to bring the Bushfire Survival Plan to\nthe annual training will need to occur by the community association.\n(12) An AQF 5 arborist inspects trees immediately adjacent to roads 17 and 18 prior\nto the bushfire danger period. Vertical distance to minimise the potential for\nfalling tree material that could hinder the movement of vehicles either through\nwind or fire impacts needs to be established.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":64},{"label":"342","code":null,"number":"342","title":"","text":"The New South Wales Rural Fire Service (NSW RFS) issued General Terms of\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 65/80\nApproval under Division 4.8 of the EPA Act 1979, and a Bush Fire Safety Authority,\ncited at [177], pursuant to s 100B of the Rural Fires Act 1997, on 10 May 2023 subject\nto seven (7) conditions.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":65},{"label":"343","code":null,"number":"343","title":"","text":"The experts agree that with the inclusion of the agreed conditions the proposal will\nsatisfy or exceed the relevant requirements of PBP and broadly resolve contention 5\n(particulars a-f inclusive).\nOther jurisdictional preconditions","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":66},{"label":"344","code":null,"number":"344","title":"","text":"Firstly I note the DA lodged with Council at [3] was made with the written consent of the\nowner of the land (Exhibit A, Tab G).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":66},{"label":"345","code":null,"number":"345","title":"","text":"The aims of the WLEP, at cl 1.2 of the WLEP, to which the Court must have regard (cl\n2.3) are in the following terms:\n“(aa) to protect and promote the use and development of land for arts and culturalactivity, including music and other performance arts,\n(a) to foster economic, environmental and social well being so that Wyong continues todevelop as a sustainable and prosperous place to live, work and visit,\n(b) to encourage a range of housing, employment, recreation, human services andappropriately located tourism-related development in Wyong to meet the existing andfuture needs of residents and visitors,\n(c) to promote the efficient and equitable provision of public services, infrastructure andamenities,\n(d) to provide for a range of local and regional community facilities for recreation,culture, health and education purposes,\n(e) to apply the principles of ecologically sustainable development to guide futuredevelopment within Wyong,\n(f) to conserve, protect and enhance the environmental and cultural heritage (bothindigenous and non-indigenous) values of Wyong,\n(g) to protect areas of high scenic landscape value,\n(h) to maintain and enhance the existing character, amenity and environmental qualityof Wyong,\n(i) to minimise risk to the community in areas subject to environmental hazards,including flooding, climate change and bush fires,\n(j) to promote a high standard of urban design that responds appropriately to theexisting or desired future character of areas,\n(k) to encourage development that increases public transport patronage, walking andcycling.”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":66},{"label":"346","code":null,"number":"346","title":"","text":"Subdivision is permitted with consent, according to cl 2.6 of the WLEP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":66},{"label":"347","code":null,"number":"347","title":"","text":"As demolition of a farmhouse and ancillary structures is proposed, cl 2.7 applies to\nrequire development consent for that purpose.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":66},{"label":"348","code":null,"number":"348","title":"","text":"As a community title scheme is proposed by the Applicant, pursuant to the Community\nLand Development Act 1989, the provisions at cl 4.1AA relevantly apply to land zoned\nR5 and C3. Subclause (3) provides that the size of any lot resulting from a subdivision\nof land is not to be less than the minimum size on the relevant lot size map.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":66},{"label":"349","code":null,"number":"349","title":"","text":"The site was subject to a Planning Proposal (Exhibit B, folio 229) that proposed, among\nother things, the rezoning of the land, and the amendment of the minimum lot size\nmaps to permit minimum lots sizes for the relevant zones comprising the site as\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 66/80\nfollows:\n(1) R5 - 1,800m2\n(2) C3 - 1,800m2\n(3) C2 - 40 hectares","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":66},{"label":"350","code":null,"number":"350","title":"","text":"To give effect to the proposal above, the Planning Proposal envisaged the insertion of a\nnew provision, identified as cl 4.1C of the WLEP, to provide for exceptions to minimum\nsubdivision lot size for certain large lot residential development.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":67},{"label":"351","code":null,"number":"351","title":"","text":"The WLEP was amended 20 July 2018 to insert cl 4.1C, that is in the following terms:\n4.1C Exceptions to minimum subdivision lot size under community titleschemes for certain large lot residential development\n(1) The objective of this clause is to enable appropriate subdivision of certain land, soas to provide for a range of lot sizes whilst retaining a predominantly rural and large lotresidential character.\n(2) This clause applies to land that is-\n(a) shown edged by a heavy red line on the Dwelling Density Map, and\n(b) wholly within Zone R5 Large Lot Residential.\n(3) Despite clauses 4.1 and 4.1AA, development consent may be granted for thesubdivision of land to which this clause applies if-\n(a) none of the lots being subdivided were created by a previous subdivisionunder this clause, and\n(b) any lot created by the subdivision is within a community title scheme underthe Community Land Development Act 1989, and\n(c) any lot created by the subdivision that has an area that is less than theminimum lot size shown on the Lot Size Map in relation to the land has aminimum area of 900 square metres, and\n(d) no more than 30% of the area of the lots created by the subdivision consistsof lots that have an area that is less than the minimum lot size shown on the LotSize Map in relation to the land, and\n(e) the development will not result in there being a total number of more than300 lots comprising land shown on the Dwelling Density Map, and\n(f) the consent authority is satisfied that the subdivision of land will not affectthe retention of the predominantly rural and large lot residential character of thelocality.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":67},{"label":"352","code":null,"number":"352","title":"","text":"The Lot Size Map applicable immediately before the site was made part of an urban\nrelease area is 20 hectares (Exhibit B, folio 728A).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":67},{"label":"353","code":null,"number":"353","title":"","text":"The Statement of Environmental Effects prepared by Stevens (Exhibit A, Tab A) cites\nthe Yield Plan (Exhibit A, Tab 26) to state that 70% of the lots resulting from the\nproposed subdivision are greater in area than 1,800m and so complies.\nHeritage","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":67},{"label":"354","code":null,"number":"354","title":"","text":"While not assisted by submissions on the subject, at least one aboriginal site appears\nto be located within the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":67},{"label":"355","code":null,"number":"355","title":"A Due Diligence Aboriginal Heritage Assessment prepared by Mary Dallas Consulting","text":"Archaeologists following an inspection of the site on 14 April 2014 records the nature of\n2\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 67/80\naboriginal site(s) on the land.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":67},{"label":"356","code":null,"number":"356","title":"An Aboriginal cultural heritage assessment report (ACHAR) prepared by McCardle","text":"Cultural Heritage dated 26 May 2022 (Exhibit A, Tab A11) records nine known\naboriginal sites are within a 2km radius of the site, with a rock shelter, art and a grinding\ngroove likely located on the site - albeit not within the site identified for development.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"357","code":null,"number":"357","title":"","text":"Accordingly, the provisions at cl 5.10 of the WLEP would appear to apply to the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"358","code":null,"number":"358","title":"The ACHAR considers the likelihood of finding aboriginal artefacts within the area","text":"proposed for development to be low due to a history of European land management\npractices such as clearing, ploughing and grazing.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"359","code":null,"number":"359","title":"The ACHAR recommends certain management practices be adopted in conditions of","text":"consent.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"360","code":null,"number":"360","title":"Having considered the effect of proposed development on heritage significance of the","text":"site in accordance with cl 5.10 of the WLEP, I note development is proposed in those\nareas of the site said to be least likely to be the site of aboriginal artefacts, and so\nconsider the proposed conditions of consent dealing with the contingency of a find\nduring the proposed works to realise development appropriate.\nFlood management","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"361","code":null,"number":"361","title":"","text":"It is commonly held that the site is flood affected. As such, the provision of cl 5.21 apply\nto the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"362","code":null,"number":"362","title":"","text":"The objectives of cl 5.21 are as follows:\n(1) The objectives of this clause are as follows-\n(a) to minimise the flood risk to life and property associated with the use ofland,\n(b) to allow development on land that is compatible with the flood function andbehaviour on the land, taking into account projected changes as a result ofclimate change,\n(c) to avoid adverse or cumulative impacts on flood behaviour and theenvironment,\n(d) to enable the safe occupation and efficient evacuation of people in the eventof a flood.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"363","code":null,"number":"363","title":"Subclause (2) precludes development consent on land the consent authority, or the","text":"Court on appeal, considers to be within the flood planning area, unless the Court is\nsatisfied of the following:\n…\n(a) is compatible with the flood function and behaviour on the land, and\n(b) will not adversely affect flood behaviour in a way that results in detrimentalincreases in the potential flood affectation of other development or properties,and\n(c) will not adversely affect the safe occupation and efficient evacuation ofpeople or exceed the capacity of existing evacuation routes for the surroundingarea in the event of a flood, and\n(d) incorporates appropriate measures to manage risk to life in the event of aflood, and\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 68/80\n(e) will not adversely affect the environment or cause avoidable erosion,siltation, destruction of riparian vegetation or a reduction in the stability of riverbanks or watercourses.\n(3) In deciding whether to grant development consent on land to which this clauseapplies, the consent authority must consider the following matters-\n(a) the impact of the development on projected changes to flood behaviour as aresult of climate change,\n(b) the intended design and scale of buildings resulting from the development,\n(c) whether the development incorporates measures to minimise the risk to lifeand ensure the safe evacuation of people in the event of a flood,\n(d) the potential to modify, relocate or remove buildings resulting fromdevelopment if the surrounding area is impacted by flooding or coastal erosion.\n(4) A word or expression used in this clause has the same meaning as it has in theConsidering Flooding in Land Use Planning Guideline unless it is otherwise defined inthis clause.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":68},{"label":"364","code":null,"number":"364","title":"","text":"I accept the agreement of the experts at [161] agree that the engineering design has\ngenerally sought to minimise the volume of imported fill to achieve the flood planning\nrequirements and drainage grades. I also accept what appears an uncontested view at\n[218] that the pool and riffle sequence and scour protection details are sufficient\nmeasures to demonstrate a level of protection against stream erosion, and that no\nadverse impact on velocities downstream result.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":69},{"label":"365","code":null,"number":"365","title":"","text":"Additionally, the flood experts agree that certain lots may be affected by the probably\nmaximum flood (Exhibit 6, pars 28-29) that may be resolved by means of a condition of\nconsent, and that modelling blockage of water flows to the vehicle crossings over Deep\nCreek of up to 50% are agreed to adequately test flood behaviour on adjacent lots\n(paras 38-40), and that scour protection is of a scale suited to the 1% AEP flows.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":69},{"label":"366","code":null,"number":"366","title":"","text":"Finally, I note the Flood Emergency Response Strategy (Exhibit A, Tab 5) proposes\nflood level warning devices designed to trigger once surrounding regional flood level\nexceeds a 5% AEP level of 7m AHD, and on-site local evacuation centre is proposed\non Lot 17.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":69},{"label":"367","code":null,"number":"367","title":"","text":"As such, I am satisfied that adequate flood planning has been demonstrated and that\nthose matters about which the Court must be satisfied at cl 5.21(2) are indeed satisfied.\nArrangements for designated State public infrastructure","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":69},{"label":"368","code":null,"number":"368","title":"","text":"An updated Planning Agreement (Exhibit B, Tab 4) from that described at [30] records\nthe agreement between Stevens and the Department of Planning to secure the\ndevelopment contribution in order to enable the Secretary to certify that satisfactory\narrangements have been made to contribute to the provision of designated State\nInfrastructure in accordance with cl 6.1 of the WLEP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":69},{"label":"369","code":null,"number":"369","title":"Email correspondence from the Department of Planning, Housing and Infrastructure","text":"(Exhibit B, Tab 3) confirms receipt of a signed hard copy of the updated planning\nagreement, and also advises that a certificate attesting the satisfactory arrangements is\npending the registration of the updated planning agreement with Land Registry\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 69/80\nServices.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":69},{"label":"370","code":null,"number":"370","title":"","text":"A certificate of a kind required by cl 6.1 of the WLEP dated 23 August 2024 certifies that\nsatisfactory arrangements have been made to contribute to the provision of designated\nState public infrastructure, supported by a plan of the proposed development at\nAttachment A (Exhibit O).\nEssential services","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"371","code":null,"number":"371","title":"Development consent is precluded unless the consent authority, or the Court on","text":"appeal, is satisfied that adequate arrangements have been made for those services at\ncl 7.9 of the WLEP to be made available when required.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"372","code":null,"number":"372","title":"","text":"Civil Engineering plans depict the conceptual layout for water and electricity and the\nalignment of, and works associated with, external works such as drainage within the\nroad reserve, road works and line marking, and supply of electricity and water prepared\nby Northrop (Exhibit A, Tabs C and O), and in those plans cited at [37].","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"373","code":null,"number":"373","title":"","text":"Email correspondence from the Council’s Water & Sewer group states that local water\nsupply and sewer management networks are capable of accommodating the demand\narising from the proposal. Connection points, pumping stations and the like are\nidentified, consistent with that proposed in the services corridor (Exhibit B, Tab 5).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"374","code":null,"number":"374","title":"","text":"Vehicular access is provided for by reference to the Civil engineering plans cited above,\nand by reference to those swept path plans for emergency access vehicles (Exhibit A,\nTab K), for buses (Exhibit A, Tab M), and for access for the electricity provider,\nTransgrid (Exhibit A, Tab J).","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"375","code":null,"number":"375","title":"","text":"As such, I am satisfied that adequate arrangements have been made for those services\nat cl 7.9 of the WLEP to be made available when required.\nAcid Sulfate Soils","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"376","code":null,"number":"376","title":"A preliminary Geotechnical and Acid Sulfate Soil Assessment prepared by Martens","text":"Consulting Engineers in March 2021 (Preliminary Assessment) (Exhibit A, Tab 17) sets\nout certain criteria and testing results for Acid Sulfate Soils (ASS) and chromium suite\nassessments, and concludes that no soil samples indicated either ASS or potential\nASS. Nevertheless, a management plan is recommended.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"377","code":null,"number":"377","title":"","text":"To this end, an ASS Management Plan prepared by Robson Civil Projects dated 29\nSeptember 2022 (Exhibit A, Tab D) likewise concludes no evidence of ASS or potential\nASS as a result of testing. However, testing indicates acidic soil originating from\nanother mechanism. As such, an unexpected finds protocol is recommended, and\ncertain controls identified at Section 4 of the ASS Management Plan.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"378","code":null,"number":"378","title":"An Unexpected Finds protocol is included in those without prejudice conditions of","text":"consent that are agreed, as is reference to both the Preliminary Assessment and ASS\nManagement Plan. As such, I am satisfied that the requirements of cl 7.1 of the WLEP\nare addressed.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 70/80\nContamination","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":70},{"label":"379","code":null,"number":"379","title":"","text":"A Detailed Site Investigation report prepared by Martens dated May 2021 (the DSI)\n(Exhibit A, Tab 21) summarises sampling results, including first and second round\ntesting that identified contaminants that were, on further investigation, deemed\nacceptable.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":71},{"label":"380","code":null,"number":"380","title":"","text":"Structures on the site that are proposed for demolition are also identified as a potential\nsource of asbestos.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":71},{"label":"381","code":null,"number":"381","title":"","text":"A Remedial Action Plan (RAP) is recommended by the DSI. A RAP has been prepared\nby Martens dated May 2021 (Exhibit A, Tab 24), outlining additional investigation to be\nundertaken to ensure remediation and validation to address data gaps and certain\ncontaminants so that the site can be made suitable for the proposed residential\ndevelopment in accordance with s 4.6(1)(b) of State Environmental Planning Policy\n(Resilience and Hazards) 2021.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":71},{"label":"382","code":null,"number":"382","title":"","text":"Provision is made in those conditions of consent that are agreed for actions identified in\nthe RAP.\nConclusion","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":71},{"label":"383","code":null,"number":"383","title":"","text":"Having considered those matters in dispute between the parties, and of the relevant\njurisdictional preconditions, I have again considered the objectives of development on\nland in the C2 and C3 zones on the site. On the basis of the measures taken by\nStevens to avoid or minimise impact of the development on the ecological values of the\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 71/80\nland and to protect those ecological communities on the site, I do not find the\ndevelopment proposed to be detrimental to the ecological values of the site, or on the\narea to which development relates.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":71},{"label":"384","code":null,"number":"384","title":"","text":"For the reasons set out above, I find the proposed development deserving of the grant\nof consent pursuant to s 4.16 of the EPA Act, subject to conditions of consent.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"385","code":null,"number":"385","title":"","text":"At the close of proceedings, the Court granted the parties leave to file and serve draft\nwithout prejudice conditions of consent no later than 30 January 2025 that would be\neither agreed, or to the extent the conditions of consent were disputed, short\nsubmissions to be provided describing the basis for that aspect of the conditions that is\nthe subject of dispute.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"386","code":null,"number":"386","title":"","text":"The parties later sought, and were granted, leave by the Court to vary that timeframe.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"387","code":null,"number":"387","title":"","text":"After the close of the Court on 4 February 2025, proposed draft conditions of consent\nwere filed by Stevens, along with short, supplementary written submissions.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"388","code":null,"number":"388","title":"","text":"On 7 February 2025, the Council filed short submissions in reply to those filed by\nStevens.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"389","code":null,"number":"389","title":"","text":"In broad terms, the conditions on which the parties are apart can be categorised as\nfollows:\n(1) Conditions in respect of the BMP.\n(2) Bus servicing\n(3) Landscape strip fronting Old Maitland Road\n(4) Evacuation centre of Lot 17\n(5) Dilapidation Report of the approved haulage route along Council roads\n(6) Survey requirements\n(7) Pet ownership\n(8) Emergency Access\n(9) Terms of the VPA in Conditions\nBMP Conditions","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"390","code":null,"number":"390","title":"","text":"In broad terms, Stevens’ submits that the conditions proposed by Council seek to\nimpose similar or identical obligations at multiple times throughout the development of\nthe subject site, and so are repetitious.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"391","code":null,"number":"391","title":"","text":"As such Council seeks to require that Stevens meet, and have certified by the Council\nthat they have met, performance targets prior to the commencement of any works or\nthe initial two-lot Torrens title subdivision - despite no performance targets applying to\nthe development under the BMP at those times and no works having been carried out.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"392","code":null,"number":"392","title":"","text":"The concern held by Stevens is that Council, through its ecologist, would hold a unique\nand unfair power to unilaterally determine whether the development can proceed, and\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 72/80\nat multiple points throughout the development.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":72},{"label":"393","code":null,"number":"393","title":"","text":"Stevens’ prefers terms that make it clear that certain information is to be submitted to\nthe Council during the development process, such as the Baseline Data Report.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"394","code":null,"number":"394","title":"","text":"I note that the terms of Stevens’ own BMP, at Section 9.2, foreshadows the issuing of\nthe Baseline Report to Council’s ecologist for approval some time prior to the release of\nthe construction certificate. The terms of Section 9.3 are such that the period\ndesignated ‘Year 1’ is to commence when Council’s Ecologist has approved the\nBaseline Report.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"395","code":null,"number":"395","title":"","text":"The Baseline Report is to be prepared by reference to Monitoring Points identified on\nFigure 4 of the BMP, but which are to be finally determined when commencing works,\nand after which monitoring is to be on a biannual basis for three years and annual\nreporting thereafter.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"396","code":null,"number":"396","title":"","text":"Stevens’ concerns are that performance targets to which reference is made in the\nterms of Condition 2.4 are not in place at such an early stage. However, as the terms of\nthe condition refer to those performance targets “as being required at this stage”, the\ncondition does not impose a requirement to demonstrate performance targets are\nachieved that are not required at that stage. The Council’s preferred form of Condition\n2.4, Condition 3.16 and 4.20 is adopted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"397","code":null,"number":"397","title":"","text":"The Council proposes a condition at Condition 6.25(d) as to regular inspections of\nartificial hollows and nesting boxes at an interval of 24 months for the first 5 years, and\nfor repair and replacement over the 20 years period of the BMP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"398","code":null,"number":"398","title":"","text":"Stevens argues that an annual inspection of artificial hollows and nesting boxes for the\nfirst 5 years and thereafter every second year for years 6-10 and the full replacement of\nall nest boxes at year 10 is sufficient.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"399","code":null,"number":"399","title":"The terms proposed by Stevens make no provision for inspection, repair of","text":"maintenance for years 11-20, being within the duration of the BMP and so the condition\nproposed by the Council at Condition 6.25(d) is preferred to align more directly with the\nperiod of the BMP.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"400","code":null,"number":"400","title":"","text":"Council also seeks to impose a condition at Condition 6.25(g) to the effect that no\nbushland is to be removed or modified, including for bushfire asset protection purposes,\nwithout the consent of Central Coast Council.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"401","code":null,"number":"401","title":"","text":"Stevens submits that to require such a covenant has the effect of setting aside the\ndetailed bushfire management regime prepared in support of the development\napplication, and is wholly unreasonable, and equates to all but a refusal of the\napplication. Even if not inconsistent with the DA and consent, such condition is\nunnecessary and unreasonable as obligations contained elsewhere in the conditions\nare sought to be duplicated by a property covenant.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"402","code":null,"number":"402","title":"","text":"As the conditions sought by the terms of the BFSA are incorporated into the conditions\nof consent, and the detailed survey sought by the Council at Condition 6.3 is adopted\n(for reasons at [439]), which allows distances between structures and vegetation\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 73/80\nsought by the RFS to be verified, I accept Stevens’ submission that the terms of\nCondition 6.25(g) as proposed by Council are susceptible to ambiguity and are deleted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":73},{"label":"403","code":null,"number":"403","title":"","text":"Stevens further submits that the requirement proposed by the Council at Condition 6.30\nthat Council’s ecologist should approve, or not approve, a report documenting\ninspections of artificial tree hollows is also problematic, and the oversight is more\nappropriately vested in the project ecologist to prepare a report on the “condition,\nfunctionality and fauna usage” of those hollows. Council’s preferred text would\nunreasonably vest power in Council’s ecologist to determine progress of development.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"404","code":null,"number":"404","title":"","text":"While I agree that there are grounds for the BMP to be submitted to the Council’s\necologist, reports prepared on those aspects of the BMP are, in my view, appropriately\noversighted by the Project ecologist. Accordingly, Stevens’ preferred text at Condition\n6.30 is adopted.\nBus servicing","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"405","code":null,"number":"405","title":"","text":"As stated at [374], the development demonstrates the internal road network is suited to\nthe bus movements through the site.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"406","code":null,"number":"406","title":"","text":"The Council proposes a condition requiring that provision be made to accommodate a\nsuitable bus route and associated transport infrastructure such as bus bay/stop/shelters\nand the like within the site prior to the issue of the Subdivision Works Certificate for\nStage 4 and 5 works.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"407","code":null,"number":"407","title":"","text":"While Stevens seeks to qualify the provision with the text “where demand from the\ndevelopment warrants the need”, the Council argues, and I agree, that such a qualifier\nis impossible to enforce as in introduces uncertainty.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"408","code":null,"number":"408","title":"Stevens has already demonstrated the site makes provision for bus movements around","text":"the site, and appears to have made progress on such provision by virtue of the\ncorrespondence from the Red Bus Services (Exhibit A, Tab R) which includes a\npossible route through the internal road network and which appears to meet with the\napproval of the possible provider. Council’s preferred form of Condition 3.13 is adopted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"409","code":null,"number":"409","title":"","text":"The Council also proposes a condition requiring a safe accessible pedestrian path of\ntravel from the development site to bus stops that service both ‘to and from’ trips at\nStage 1.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"410","code":null,"number":"410","title":"","text":"Steven’s argues that as there is no internal bus service in Stage 1, but that future bus\nstops are proposed internal to the site, those footpaths are proposed throughout the\ndevelopment.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"411","code":null,"number":"411","title":"","text":"I understand the Council to require no more than the provision of pathways to a future\nservice, at a time prior to the need arising. The provision of a path to a bus stop is not,\nin my mind, necessarily shackled to the timing of the bus service itself. Rather, such a\ncondition ensures a footpath is installed ahead of, and not after, the bus service is\nmade active. Council’s preferred form of Condition 6.28 is adopted.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 74/80\nLandscape strip fronting Old Maitland Road","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":74},{"label":"412","code":null,"number":"412","title":"","text":"As stated at [178], the landscape frontage to Old Maitland Road is acceptable subject\nto the terms of a condition as to its maintenance and management.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":75},{"label":"413","code":null,"number":"413","title":"At condition 3.14, the Council proposes terms that require amendments to the","text":"approved plans that must detail:\nThe Landscape strip fronting Old Maitland Road being included in the\nAssociation property under the plan of subdivision.\nLandscaping detailed design plans for the landscaping along Old Maitland Road\nnominating all proposed species to be planted and existing trees to be retained\nand protected within the buffer area.\nPost and rail fencing shall be erected on the front property boundary along Old\nMaitland Road and along the easement line at the 14m setback. Fencing plan\nshowing the landscape buffer along Old Maitland Road within each proposed lot\nalong this site frontage.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":75},{"label":"414","code":null,"number":"414","title":"","text":"That said, Council also accepts that the landscape strip can remain in private\nownership but should be managed by the Community Association. Allowing owners to\nfail at managing the main interface with the public domain before the Community\nAssociation steps in is unacceptable.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":75},{"label":"415","code":null,"number":"415","title":"","text":"I do not accept that the terms of the Community Management Statement (Exhibit A ,Tab\nA9) adequately provides for owners of Lots 7-17 to manage their portion of the\nlandscape strip fronting Old Maitland Road.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":75},{"label":"416","code":null,"number":"416","title":"By-law 7.1 of the Community Management Statement limits the responsibility of the","text":"Community Association to Community Property. Stevens objects to the landscape strip\nbeing included as Community Association property under the plan of subdivision.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":75},{"label":"417","code":null,"number":"417","title":"While landscape plans adequately document the arrangement of landscape in this","text":"frontage, and depict a post and rail fence, the condition does not seek to address the\nnature of the landscape as installed, but the means by which it is maintained.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":75},{"label":"418","code":null,"number":"418","title":"","text":"Having considered the expert evidence at [167]-[177], the terms of a condition that\nmost accurately reflects the agreement reached is as follows:\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 75/80\nStage 1 Submit amendments to the Community Management Statement that mustdesignate the landscape strip fronting Old Maitland Road as an area under the control,management, operation, maintenance and repair property under the plan of subdivision.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":75},{"label":"419","code":null,"number":"419","title":"","text":"As the landscape strip also serves a bush fire function, the Council seeks to impose a\ncondition requiring a ‘Restriction on the use of Land’ over lots 7 to 17 restricting the\nerection of any structure within the Inner Protection Area, landscaping area and\neasement area which extends 14m from the front (eastern) property boundary.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"420","code":null,"number":"420","title":"","text":"As stated at [169], Lot 13, within the sequence otherwise referred to as ‘Lot 7-17’ does\nnot front Old Maitland Road, and does not have land to which the landscape strip\napplies.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"421","code":null,"number":"421","title":"","text":"Stevens does not object to the condition, other than to propose substituting the term\n‘structure’ with the term ‘dwelling’ so that the condition does not preclude the installation\nof fences, water features and ponds, minor landscaping structures, bird feeders, sheds,\ncabanas and the like.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"422","code":null,"number":"422","title":"","text":"When the terms of the BFSA, and the requirements of an Internal Protection Area are\nproperly understood, it is my view that a clear and unambiguous condition that\nprecludes obstructions within the IPA to be the preferred position of the RFS.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"423","code":null,"number":"423","title":"","text":"As such, I consider it appropriate to substantially adopt the text preferred by the Council\nin Condition 6.24(l), but for the lot references, seeking to create a ‘Restriction on the\nuse of Land’ over lots 7-12 and 14-17 restricting the erection of any structure within the\nInner Protection Area, landscaping area and easement area which extends 14m from\nthe front (eastern) property boundary.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"424","code":null,"number":"424","title":"","text":"Consequentially, condition 6.25(a) is also to be amended to read:\na) The Community Association shall manage the 5m landscape buffer across Lots 7-12and 14-17\nEvacuation centre on Lot 17","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"425","code":null,"number":"425","title":"","text":"The Council proposes a condition at Condition 3.15 requiring, prior to the issue of the\nsubdivision works certificate for Stage 4, that a development application must be\nsubmitted and approved, and an occupation certificate issued for the proposed flood\nevacuation centre on Lot 17. The evacuation centre must be sized to have a minimum\nfloor area of 350 m² for flood refuge and must be permanently equipped with at least\nfour toilets and four showers (with at least one disabled bathroom/shower facility), a\nbattery powered generator, defibrillator, first aid kit and bottled water. The building shall\ninclude a permanent metal plaque near the front door that is legible to explain that it is\na flood evacuation centre. A positive covenant is to be applied to the building such that\nits purpose as a flood evacuation centre is managed in perpetuity.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"426","code":null,"number":"426","title":"","text":"Stevens seeks to insert within the condition certain provisions allowing for its use as a\ncommunity centre outside of emergency flood events.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"427","code":null,"number":"427","title":"","text":"However, the Council submits that an insertion of such text would have the effect of\napproving the use of the emergency evacuation centre as a separate and distinct use,\nbeing a community centre, absent a proposal in the development application for such a\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 76/80\nuse.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":76},{"label":"428","code":null,"number":"428","title":"","text":"I accept the submission that passing reference to use of the flood evacuation centre as\na community centre outside of those periods when the flood evacuation centre serves\nthat purpose infers a use for which consent is entertained, absent a Plan of\nManagement as to the terms of such a use.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"429","code":null,"number":"429","title":"","text":"The Council’s preferred form of Condition 3.15 is adopted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"430","code":null,"number":"430","title":"","text":"It is relevant to note here that the proposed evacuation centre is not a dwelling, but is\nproposed to be managed as a structure for the purposes of the Internal Protection Area.\nDilapidation Report of the approved haulage route along Council roads","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"431","code":null,"number":"431","title":"","text":"Consistent with the Council’s position at [256], a condition is proposed to require a\ndilapidation assessment to assess the additional impacts arising from the proposal, and\nfor that report to be prepared across the haulage route in the LGA.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"432","code":null,"number":"432","title":"","text":"The terms initially proposed by the Council are amended in response to submissions by\nStevens that seek to distinguish between Council assets and those roads that are not\nCouncil assets.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"433","code":null,"number":"433","title":"","text":"The Council’s preferred text, as amended, reads:\nSubmit to Council, for approval, details of proposed haulage routes to and from the site,within the vicinity of the development Council LGA. Approval of haulage routes may besubject to the provision of a dilapidation report of all or part of the approved haulageroute along Council roads Any dilapidation document is to provide photographs thatclearly depict any existing damage to the road, kerb, gutter, footpath, driveways, watersupply, sewer works, street trees, street signs or any other Council assets in the vicinityof the development.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"434","code":null,"number":"434","title":"","text":"Whether the quantum of truck movements are those set out at [255], or the lesser\nnumber that would result from the measures at [260], I accept that construction related\nimpacts are not excluded from those impacts of the development likely to be\ncharacterised as off-site impacts. As such, those impacts are matters of relevance to\nthe development the subject of the development application under s 4.15(1)(b) of the\nEPA Act.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"435","code":null,"number":"435","title":"","text":"A dilapidation report of a kind proposed by the Council merely seeks to assess and\nrecord the state of local roads and associated infrastructure prior to the imposition of\nthose truck movements required to deliver fill. As such, the Council’s amended form of\nthe condition proposed at Condition 4.13 is adopted.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"436","code":null,"number":"436","title":"","text":"Relatedly, I note the without prejudice conditions of consent make provision for certain\nmatters to be the subject of a revised Construction Traffic Management Plan (CTMP).\nHowever, those recommendations at [260] are not currently listed among the matters to\nbe incorporated into a CTMP. As I have accepted those recommendations will assist in\nachieving acceptable acoustic levels to residents along Old Maitland Road, it is\nnecessary to include those at Condition 4.10.\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 77/80\nSurvey","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":77},{"label":"437","code":null,"number":"437","title":"","text":"Prior to the issue of any Subdivision Certificate, the Council seeks to require\ncertification prepared by a Registered Surveyor certifying that all construction has been\neffected within the appropriate property, easement boundaries and rights of\ncarriageway. The certification must be accompanied by a copy of the final subdivision\nor easement plan, with the distances from the boundaries to the edges of these\nstructures endorsed in red and signed by the surveyor.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"438","code":null,"number":"438","title":"","text":"Stevens argues that, absent a reason to impose such a burden, the condition should\nrequire no more than a survey certifying that all infrastructure has been constructed\nwithin the appropriate lots and, where required, are appropriately addressed by suitable\neasements and rights of carriageway.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"439","code":null,"number":"439","title":"","text":"The essential difference is the extent of aspects to be surveyed, and the information\ncontained on the survey. I do not consider the extent proposed by Council to be\nburdensome as asserted by Stevens, and provides for a greater degree of certainty as\nthe construction of elements on the site as an appropriate prerequisite to the issue of a\nSubdivision Certificate. The Council’s preferred text at condition 6.3 is adopted.\nPet ownership","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"440","code":null,"number":"440","title":"The Council seeks to impose conditions precluding cats from the development while","text":"Stevens seeks to rely on terms within the Community Management Statement that\nincludes a requirement that cats be kept indoors and/or in enclosed outdoor cat runs.\nSuch a requirement is enforceable and, an outright ban is excessive and unreasonable.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"441","code":null,"number":"441","title":"","text":"The Council argues that the containment of cats is impossible and so cannot be\npermitted in a location adjoining a stewardship site, particularly where opening of doors\nin the summer months is common.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"442","code":null,"number":"442","title":"","text":"While it is reasonable to expect residents to comply with conditions of consent, I accept\nthe balancing act necessary when considering the terms of residential development on\na site adjoining the Stewardship site with the aim of successful regeneration of a native\necosystem, and a BMP that likewise seeks to restore a VRZ along the alignment of\nCreek B. In considering that balancing act, and the reasonable expectation that owners\nwill comply with conditions of consent, I also accept that the ecological values of the\nsite and the area are served by adopting Council’s preferred text at Condition 6.24(s).\nEmergency Access","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"443","code":null,"number":"443","title":"The Council seeks the proposed emergency access road to be constructed and","text":"operational, by which it means the road should be capable of use, prior to the release\nof the Subdivision Certificate for Stage 1.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"444","code":null,"number":"444","title":"","text":"Stevens argues that the emergency access road cannot be operational prior to the\nissue of a Subdivision Certificate. I accept Council’s clarification of the term\n‘operational’ and, so understood, I find no reason to amend the condition proposed by\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 78/80\nthe Council at Condition 6.21.\nTerms of the VPA in Conditions","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":78},{"label":"445","code":null,"number":"445","title":"","text":"The Council seeks the imposition of terms at condition 4.3 to ensure that the\nbiobanking site, or the Stewardship Site, is secured prior to works commencing. Absent\nthis provision the securing of the land under a biobanking agreement is not attached to\nany other obligation. A VPA has not been finalised with Council. This condition ensures\nthe land is secured.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":79},{"label":"446","code":null,"number":"446","title":"","text":"Stevens submits the condition is otiose as the issue is address by the terms of\nCondition 2.2. Condition 2.2 is as follows:\nPrior to the commencement of any works associated with stages 1 to 5 of theCommunity Plan of Subdivision, the proposed Biodiversity Stewardship Site that occursover the adjacent C2 zoned land on part Lot 1/DP554423, Lot 36/DP755249, Lot41/DP123953, Lot 1/DP229971, Lot 1/DP229970, part Lot 1/ DP120512 and Lot101/DP604655 as identified in the draft Biodiversity Stewardship Site AssessmentReport (BSSAR), prepared by Anderson Environment and Planning (internal ref:1910.06, BOAMS case BAAS18147/21/00025873) must be fully operational. Thisincludes the retirement of all ecosystem and species credits, the establishment of theTotal Fund Deposit and the commencement of management actions identified in theBSSAR.","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":79},{"label":"447","code":null,"number":"447","title":"","text":"I agree with Stevens that the issue would appear dealt with by the terms of Condition\n2.2. However, I also accept that the terms of Condition 4.3 are not inconsistent with\nthose at Condition 2.2 and no harm arises by its inclusion.\nGeneral Terms of Approval - Natural Resources Access Regulator","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":79},{"label":"448","code":null,"number":"448","title":"","text":"While not a matter in dispute, as stated briefly at [232], the Natural Resources Access\nRegulator (NRAR) issued general terms of approval dated 21 September 2021. Those\nterms are as follows:\n“TC-G001 Before commencing any proposed controlled activity on waterfront land, anapplication must be submitted to Natural Resources Access Regulator, and obtained,for a controlled activity approval under the Water Management Act 2000.\nTC-G004 A. The General Terms of Approval (GTA) only applies to the proposedcontrolled activity described in the plans and associated documents found in Schedule1, relating to Development Application DA765/2021 CNR-26521 A-30957 provided byCouncil to Natural Resources Access Regulator.\nB. Any Amendments or modifications to the proposed controlled activity may render theGTA invalid. If the proposed controlled activity is amended or modified, NaturalResources Access Regulator, must be notified in writing to determine if any variations tothe GTA will be required.\nTC-G005 A. The application for a controlled activity approval must include thefollowing:\ni. Site plans indicating the demarcation of waterfront land, designatedriparian corridors and identifying any areas of encroachments andoffsets\nii. Detailed civil construction plans with an average 20m riparian zone;\niii. Drainage Plans;\niv. Erosion and sediment control plans;\nv. Vegetation management plan\nvi. vii. (sic) Construction streamworks plans; viii. Constructionwatercourse crossing design plans;\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 79/80\nB. The plan(s) must be prepared in accordance with Natural ResourcesAccess Regulator’s guidelines located on the website\nhttps://wwwdpie.nsw.gov.au/nrar/how-to-apply/controlledactivities/guidelines-for-controlled-activities”","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":79},{"label":"449","code":null,"number":"449","title":"The letter accompanying the General Terms of Approval (GTAs) states that Council’s","text":"statutory obligations under s 4.46 of the EPA Act requires any consent to be consistent\nwith the general terms of approval proposed by an approval body. Consistent with the\nNatural Resources Access Regulator request that the GTAs set out above are included\nin any proposed development consent, condition 1.3 of the without prejudice conditions\nof consent require Stevens to comply with the GTAs set out above.\nOrders","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":80},{"label":"450","code":null,"number":"450","title":"","text":"The Court orders that:\n(1) The Applicant is granted leave to rely on those amended plans and other\ndocuments listed in Annexure A, subject to paying the costs of the Respondent\nthrown away as agreed or assessed in accordance with s 8.15(3) of the\nEnvironmental Planning and Assessment Act 1979.\n(2) The appeal is upheld.\n(3) Development application DA/765/2021 for community title subdivision, carried\nout in five stages, to create 182 residential lots including associated servicing\nworks, roadworks, earthworks, demolition and landscaping at 414 Old Maitland\nRoad, Mardi is determined by the grant of consent, subject to those conditions\nof consent at Annexure B.\n(4) All Exhibits are returned, except for Exhibits A, B, E, M and 9.\nT Horton\nCommissioner of the Court\n**********\n57547.23 Annexure A (107 KB, pdf)\n57547.23 Annexure B (590 KB, pdf)\nDISCLAIMER - Every effort has been made to comply with suppression orders or statutory provisionsprohibiting publication that may apply to this judgment or decision. The onus remains on any personusing material in the judgment or decision to ensure that the intended use of that material does notbreach any such order or provision. Further enquiries may be directed to the Registry of the Court orTribunal in which it was generated.\nDecision last updated: 30 May 2025\n02/06/2025, 10:57 Stevens Holdings Pty Limited v Central Coast Council - NSW Caselaw\nhttps://www.caselaw.nsw.gov.au/decision/197103a83655e3818e79ed5a 80/80","stage":"unassigned","stage_basis":"unassigned","page":80}]});
